window.WLAcademicDocuments={"L1.S1":"<div class=\"eyebrow\">Academic Edition 3 · Candidate</div><h1>L1.S1 — The Wealth Equation</h1><div class=\"notice\">Additive textbook supplement. Your live app, progress, bookmarks and legacy assessment positions have not been changed.</div><div class=\"intro\"><h1>L1.S1 — The Wealth Equation</h1>\n<p><strong>Academic reconstruction · Supplement to existing Gold Edition 2 · Candidate Edition 3</strong><br />\n<strong>Discipline:</strong> Household financial accounting, economic measurement, financial decision-making.<br />\n<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Percentages; addition, subtraction and signed numbers; understanding what a debt is. The existing six introductory and six Gold Edition 2 reading parts remain prerequisites and are not replaced.<br />\n<strong>Learning outcomes:</strong> Prepare and reconcile a dated household statement of financial position; distinguish stocks from flows; value household assets conservatively; classify liabilities, contingent obligations and liquidity restrictions; explain the mechanics of a dozen transactions with the accounting equation; compute solvency, liquidity, leverage and runway ratios; construct a defensible housing recommendation under multiple scenarios.</p>\n</div><div class=\"lab\" data-lab=\"L1.S1\"><h3>Try It · Mathematical learning lab</h3><p>Local demonstration only; no real financial data, no saving, and no credit or investment guarantee.</p><div class=\"controls\"><label>Cash deposits ($)<input data-v=\"cash\" type=\"number\" value=\"11400\" min=\"0\"></label><label>Home estimated value ($)<input data-v=\"home\" type=\"number\" value=\"245000\" min=\"0\"></label><label>Other assets ($)<input data-v=\"other\" type=\"number\" value=\"31900\" min=\"0\"></label><label>Mortgage ($)<input data-v=\"mort\" type=\"number\" value=\"217500\" min=\"0\"></label><label>Other debt ($)<input data-v=\"debt\" type=\"number\" value=\"12400\" min=\"0\"></label><label>Home price stress (%)<input data-v=\"shock\" type=\"number\" value=\"-10\" step=\"1\"></label></div><div class=\"results\"><div class=\"metric\"><span>Assets today</span><b data-out=\"assets\"></b></div><div class=\"metric\"><span>Liabilities</span><b data-out=\"liabilities\"></b></div><div class=\"metric\"><span>Current net worth</span><b data-out=\"networth\"></b></div><div class=\"metric\"><span>Net worth after home move</span><b data-out=\"stress\"></b></div></div></div><div class=\"academic\"><section id=\"L1.S1.A1\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S1.A1</div><h2>A. The question that financial statements answer</h2>\n<p>Imagine two households. Household A earns $110,000 per year but has high fixed expenses, negative home equity, little cash and variable employment. Household B earns $55,000 per year, owns a modest paid-off property and holds accessible reserves. Which is wealthier? There is no safe answer from income alone. We need several measurements, each tied to a clearly defined question and date. Wealth is not identical to earnings, a credit score, a checking-account balance, or the feeling that assets are valuable.</p>\n<p>A household <strong>statement of financial position</strong> (informally, a personal balance sheet) measures the economic claims and obligations at one date. An income-and-expense report measures what happened over a period. A cash forecast measures whether and when money can settle obligations. Financial well-being also includes the ability to withstand shocks and make meaningful choices, a broader concept than net worth. The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau explicitly treats present control, shock absorption, goal progress and freedom of choice as separate dimensions of financial well-being (CFPB: https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/financial-well-being/about/; accessed 2026-11-01).</p>\n<p>Financial professionals avoid replacing one measure with another. A balance sheet can be positive while the household faces immediate insolvency in cash terms. A paycheck can increase cash without increasing wealth if the money is borrowed rather than earned. A home can appreciate while the owner cannot sell it quickly. Every report must carry a measurement date, valuation assumptions, currency units, ownership boundary and a note identifying what is uncertain.</p>\n</section><section id=\"L1.S1.A2\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S1.A2</div><h2>B. The accounting identity and its derivation</h2>\n<p>For a household, define <strong>A</strong> as total economically valued assets, <strong>L</strong> as existing liabilities and <strong>W</strong> as residual net worth. Assets are resources owned or controlled that carry economic value; liabilities are obligations payable to other parties. The owner’s residual claim is what remains after satisfying creditors. Therefore:</p>\n<p>[A = L + W]</p>\n<p>Subtract L from both sides to derive (W=A-L). This is an accounting identity, not an estimate of how much cash could be withdrawn, or a forecast of investment returns. If a home worth $245,000 secures a $217,500 mortgage, the gross home asset is $245,000, the debt is $217,500, and home equity is $27,500 before selling costs. Report the gross figures in their proper categories rather than reporting the equity and then subtracting the mortgage a second time.</p>\n<p>At the transaction level, classify each change using (\\Delta A=\\Delta L+\\Delta W). When $2,000 is borrowed and deposited into checking, (\\Delta A=+2,000), (\\Delta L=+2,000), (\\Delta W=0). When $600 of existing savings pays off loan principal, (\\Delta A=-600), (\\Delta L=-600), so wealth is unchanged <strong>at the moment of repayment</strong>. When $60 of interest is paid from savings, (\\Delta A=-60), (\\Delta L=0), so wealth falls by $60. When labor produces $1,500 of new take-home cash and no other transaction occurs, (\\Delta A=+1,500) and (\\Delta W=+1,500). This last description simplifies payroll taxes and withholding because it examines net cash wages and ignores related receivables or payables.</p>\n<p>A transaction can affect wealth without moving cash. If a vehicle's realizable market value falls from $14,000 to $12,000, a mark-to-market personal balance sheet loses $2,000 of assets and net worth, even if the vehicle never changes hands. Accounting conventions for a business are not always the same as household estimates: an accountant may record historical cost and depreciation, whereas a personal financial planner may use present resale value. The analyst must identify which convention is being used and keep it consistent.</p>\n</section><section id=\"L1.S1.A3\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S1.A3</div><h2>C. Build a source-backed household statement</h2>\n<p>The following is a <strong>fictional, single-date Connecticut case</strong> at October 31, 2026. It is not a statement of any user's personal finances. Every amount is hypothetical and expressed in US dollars. Students must name an evidence source beside every balance.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"text-align:left\">Assets</th>\n<th style=\"text-align:right\">Estimated value</th>\n<th style=\"text-align:left\">Evidence and valuation basis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Checking</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$4,200</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Bank available/current balance after reconciliation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Savings</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$7,200</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Deposit statement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Retirement investments</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$18,500</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Same-day brokerage statement; market value, not spendable cash</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Vehicle</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$13,400</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Reasonable resale range, not original purchase price</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Connecticut home</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$245,000</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Supported appraisal/market range, not a guaranteed sale receipt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\"><strong>Total assets</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>$288,300</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Liabilities</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">Balance</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Mortgage</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$217,500</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Servicer principal/payoff information; verify accrued items</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Auto loan</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$8,500</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Loan statement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Credit card</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$2,400</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Reconciled card statement, including posted charges</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Outstanding tax payable</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$1,500</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Separately substantiated, not double-counted withholding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\"><strong>Total liabilities</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>$229,900</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\"><strong>Net worth</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>$58,400</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">$288,300 − $229,900</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The home accounts for $245,000 of assets, approximately 85.0% of total assets, but that concentration is not a claim that it can be liquidated at its stated value. The vehicle's market value may be estimated rather than known; a reasonable range such as $12,000–$14,000 could move net worth by ±$1,000 relative to the $13,000 midpoint. A technically correct sum can still be economically misleading if the estimates are unjustified. Label assumptions and distinguish high-confidence statement balances from low-confidence market valuations.</p>\n<p><strong>Financial-statement quality control:</strong> (1) identify the valuation date; (2) document ownership shares and whether jointly held accounts are counted once; (3) remove duplicate bank-to-bank transfers; (4) include outstanding obligations not visible in the bank feed; (5) separate debts actually owed from contingent guarantees; (6) do not count unused credit-card limits as assets; (7) choose either gross asset/gross debt or net equity presentation and never mix them. The SEC's Investor.gov describes the personal asset-minus-liability statement as a net-worth statement (https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/save-and-invest/figure-out-your-finances; accessed 2026-11-01).</p>\n</section><section id=\"L1.S1.A4\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S1.A4</div><h2>D. Going from a bank statement to solvency and liquidity</h2>\n<p><strong>Solvency</strong> asks whether the household's assets exceed its liabilities under a specified valuation method. <strong>Liquidity</strong> asks whether appropriate assets can settle specific obligations on time at acceptable cost. Solvency is a point-in-time stock; liquidity requires a dated schedule and knowledge of withdrawal restrictions, settlement periods, penalties and reserve commitments. These can move in opposite directions: paying $5,000 in cash to reduce mortgage principal does not mechanically raise net worth, but may meaningfully reduce immediate liquidity.</p>\n<p>For the sample household, nominal cash deposits equal $11,400. Suppose $3,600 is reserved for housing and food already due and $1,600 is committed to an upcoming tax/insurance bill. <strong>Uncommitted accessible cash</strong> is $11,400 − $3,600 − $1,600 = <strong>$6,200</strong>. The committed cash is still an asset; it is not available for a second purpose. This is an allocation problem, not a redefinition of net worth. Retirement assets may be part of wealth without being suitable for near-term bills because of market volatility, taxes and account rules.</p>\n<p>A descriptive <strong>liquid coverage ratio</strong> can be defined as uncommitted accessible cash divided by a clearly specified set of near-term obligations. With $6,200 uncommitted and a hypothetical $3,100 of essential expenses over the following month, coverage is 2.00 months <strong>only if</strong> those amounts are not already covered elsewhere and spending occurs evenly enough for the approximation to be useful. For due-date decisions, construct a cash calendar instead. Ratios are aids to judgment; no universal ratio proves that a household is safe.</p>\n<p>A <strong>liabilities-to-assets ratio</strong> is (L/A). The sample yields 229,900/288,300 = about 79.7%. This represents leverage on the modeled valuation, not a lender-defined debt-to-income ratio. A <strong>net-worth-to-assets ratio</strong> is (W/A), about 20.3%; the two add to 100% when A is positive. These ratios can turn misleading if the home is significantly overvalued or a jointly owned asset is included twice. Unlike mortgage LTV, total leverage mixes multiple kinds of assets and debts; label the denominator each time.</p>\n<h3>A transaction bridge, not a pile of unconnected snapshots</h3>\n<p>Start with $50,000 in assets and $20,000 in liabilities; net worth is $30,000. Then record six isolated transactions, in order, using the same valuation method:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"text-align:left\">Transaction</th>\n<th style=\"text-align:right\">Δ assets</th>\n<th style=\"text-align:right\">Δ liabilities</th>\n<th style=\"text-align:right\">Δ net worth</th>\n<th style=\"text-align:right\">Closing net worth</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Earn net wages and save $2,500</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">+$2,500</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$0</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">+$2,500</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$32,500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Consume $500 of cash</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">−$500</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$0</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">−$500</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$32,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Repay $1,000 of loan principal</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">−$1,000</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">−$1,000</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$0</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$32,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Borrow $2,000 and retain proceeds</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">+$2,000</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">+$2,000</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$0</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$32,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Revalue an investment down $300</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">−$300</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$0</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">−$300</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$31,700</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Pay $100 loan interest</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">−$100</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$0</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">−$100</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>$31,600</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The check is (A_1-L_1=W_1) after <strong>every</strong> row, not only at the end. If the identity fails, a transaction may be classified incorrectly. Observe that paying principal and borrowing change balance-sheet composition, while interest and consumption create economic expenses. Wealth is not simply all deposits into checking minus all withdrawals.</p>\n</section><section id=\"L1.S1.A5\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S1.A5</div><h2>E. Home purchase and the transaction-cost problem</h2>\n<p>Consider a fictional buyer with $25,000 savings, no other assets or debt in the isolated illustration. A $250,000 Connecticut home requires a 5% down payment ($12,500), a $237,500 mortgage and <strong>illustrative</strong> nonrecoverable transaction costs of $7,500. Immediately before closing, wealth is $25,000. Immediately after closing, cash is $5,000, house value is $250,000 and mortgage debt is $237,500. Net worth is $5,000 + $250,000 − $237,500 = $17,500. The down payment changed the <strong>form</strong> of wealth; transaction costs reduced it by $7,500. Real closings may have escrow/prepaid items that remain economic assets for a time, concessions, or fees financed through debt, so individual lines must be classified separately instead of automatically expensing every amount paid at closing.</p>\n<p>The mortgage debt creates <strong>leverage</strong>: the buyer controls a $250,000 asset using $12,500 initial equity, but debt claims retain priority if market value falls. A 10% home-price decline brings modeled value to $225,000 and equity to −$12,500 if the principal has not changed. If selling costs are 6% of sale price ($13,500), then net sale proceeds after repaying the $237,500 mortgage are −$26,000. This means the owner would need roughly $26,000 from elsewhere to complete the modeled sale, assuming no other charges or liens. It is not a prediction of Connecticut house prices or actual sale fees.</p>\n<p>A different decision may be better even when monthly mortgage principal and interest appear affordable. The buyer would leave with $5,000 cash; if expected monthly essential costs total $3,800, that is only about 1.32 months of expenses before distinguishing committed amounts. A $3,000 furnace repair consumes most of it. The CFPB's mortgage disclosures distinguish principal/interest from insurance, taxes and total projected payment; the Loan Estimate also addresses upfront cash needed (https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/loan-estimate/; accessed 2026-11-01). Our lesson does <strong>not</strong> infer lender approval or down-payment-assistance eligibility from these simplified calculations.</p>\n</section><section id=\"L1.S1.A6\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S1.A6</div><h2>F. Valuation uncertainty, double counting and financial judgment</h2>\n<p>Assets need not be equally uncertain. Cash is denominated in dollars but still has access and insurance considerations. A car is often an estimated resale asset; transaction and repair costs make a Kelley-Blue-Book-style estimate different from settled cash. Homes face market, title, environmental and selling-cost risks. A business interest may have book value but be difficult to sell. A life-insurance death benefit is generally <strong>not</strong> an owned cash asset payable during the insured's life; certain permanent policies may have contract-specific cash surrender value, which must be considered net of relevant charges and loan balances, without counting the same economic value twice.</p>\n<p>A rigorous personal statement should include an <strong>uncertainty register</strong>: item, low/base/high valuation, confidence, date, potential selling costs, accessibility, and whether the household needs a legal or tax specialist. For example, if the home estimate is $230,000–$255,000 rather than exactly $245,000, mortgage principal $217,500 yields gross equity between $12,500 and $37,500 before sale costs. This range is more informative than treating $27,500 as a guaranteed cash resource. One should also flag contingent guaranties and co-signed loans; they may pose serious risks even if payment is not currently due, while their accounting recognition depends on the purpose of the statement.</p>\n<h3>Independent homework — do before viewing the key</h3>\n<p><strong>P1 — Classification and reconciliation (15 points).</strong> Cash $7,500, savings $4,800, retirement $32,000, car $12,000, home $280,000; mortgage $245,000, auto debt $9,800, card $3,100. Compute A, L and W. A credit card company raises the available limit by $4,000: does W change? Show the resulting accounting identity.</p>\n<p><strong>P2 — Transaction effects (15 points).</strong> Starting from P1, the household (i) takes a $2,000 personal loan, (ii) spends $600 on groceries, (iii) uses $900 cash to repay card principal, (iv) pays $45 of interest. Show (\\Delta A, \\Delta L, \\Delta W) for each, then reconcile the closing equation.</p>\n<p><strong>P3 — Quality of evidence (10 points).</strong> Explain why the following three entries cannot be blindly added: a jointly owned house shown in both partners' apps, a pending $400 card authorization already included in posted card debt, and the face amount of a term life policy. Explain exactly what evidence you would seek.</p>\n<p><strong>P4 — Housing downside (15 points).</strong> Fictional purchase price $270,000, 5% down, 3% <strong>nonrecoverable</strong> costs. Compute starting mortgage, immediate equity, net-worth transaction-cost effect, equity after a 12% decline assuming no amortization, and amount needed to close a sale at the declined value with hypothetical 5% selling costs. State two missing real-world inputs.</p>\n<p><strong>P5 — Judgment memo (15 points).</strong> A household owns a $320,000 home, owes $190,000, holds $1,000 accessible cash and faces $3,400 required expenses before its next pay. Defend or reject the claim, 'This household can afford a $2,000 investment because it has $130,000 home equity.' Give a payment-timing remedy that does not assume guaranteed new borrowing.</p>\n<p><strong>P6 — Source investigation and sensitivity (20 points).</strong> Obtain a real official loan-estimate explainer from the CFPB. Identify at least three distinct disclosed figures that a balance-sheet-only comparison would overlook. For a $250,000 home, calculate net proceeds after $220,000 outstanding debt and 5% selling costs at three prices: $225,000, $250,000 and $275,000. State why the cost percentage is only an assumption.</p>\n<p><strong>P7 — Written argument (10 points).</strong> Evaluate: 'Paying $10,000 toward the mortgage makes me $10,000 richer.' Explain the difference between wealth, interest savings and cash resilience; state under what conditions paying principal may improve a future outcome.</p>\n<details><summary>Show worked solutions &amp; answer explanations</summary><h3>Answer key and scoring rationale</h3>\n<p><strong>P1.</strong> A=7,500+4,800+32,000+12,000+280,000=$336,300. L=245,000+9,800+3,100=$257,900. W=$78,400. Unused card credit is borrowing capacity, so all three are unchanged when the limit rises. Full credit requires both math and a reason for excluding the limit.</p>\n<p><strong>P2.</strong> (i) +2,000 assets, +2,000 liabilities, 0 wealth. (ii) −600 assets, 0 liabilities, −600 wealth. (iii) −900 assets, −900 liabilities, 0 wealth. (iv) −45 assets, 0 liabilities, −45 wealth. Closing A=$336,755, L=$259,000, W=$77,755; 336,755−259,000=77,755. Full credit requires a separate row and reconciliation.</p>\n<p><strong>P3.</strong> Count only the relevant household ownership interest once; compare pending and posted charges to avoid duplicate liability recognition; a term-policy face death benefit is not normally accessible cash value during the insured's lifetime. Obtain deed/ownership evidence, issuer transaction records and the policy contract. Strong answers describe the reporting boundary, not just 'avoid double counting.'</p>\n<p><strong>P4.</strong> Down=$13,500; loan=$256,500; costs=$8,100. Gross starting equity=$13,500; costs reduce immediate modeled W by $8,100. Declined price=$237,600; gross equity=$237,600−$256,500=−$18,900. Selling costs=$11,880; net sale amount after payoff is −$30,780. Lender payoff including accrual, tax/proration, actual sale fees, repair needs and financing terms are missing. Full marks require the distinction between price loss and nonrecoverable costs.</p>\n<p><strong>P5.</strong> Equity is $130,000 but the $2,400 near-term liquidity gap exists before any investment. Do not assume that the equity can fund the expense. A defensible alternative is to postpone new investment, identify contractually negotiable due dates before delinquency, coordinate pay timing, reduce nonessential short-term outflows or sell genuinely liquid nonessential property; quantify each proposed change. A high net worth is not proof of payment capacity.</p>\n<p><strong>P6.</strong> CFPB examples: estimated total monthly payment, taxes/insurance/escrow, cash to close, loan costs, adjustable-rate features; cite the exact section reviewed. Prices and modeled net amounts: $225,000 sale: 225,000×0.95−220,000=−$6,250; $250,000 sale: 237,500−220,000=$17,500; $275,000 sale: 261,250−220,000=$41,250. 5% is a hypothetical commission/all-in-cost simplifying assumption, not a required statewide fee.</p>\n<p><strong>P7.</strong> Immediate cash and mortgage liability both fall $10,000; net worth does not increase merely because principal was repaid. Future interest charges may fall and optional terms could change the payment schedule, but cash available for shocks falls today. Strong answers distinguish timing and opportunity cost; they do not claim automatic monthly payment reductions.</p>\n</details><h3>Academic completion gate for L1.S1</h3>\n<p>Award up to 100 points. Require ≥80 overall, ≥70 on quantitative P1/P2/P4/P6 combined, and acceptable reasoning on P5/P7. A correct formula without units, valuation date, stated assumptions or a reconciliation check loses method points. Learners revise unsuccessful work and explain the correction. Multiple-choice answers alone never establish mastery.</p>\n<p><strong>Primary references (retrieved 2026-11-01):</strong> SEC Investor.gov personal net worth statement; CFPB financial well-being and CFPB Loan Estimate explainers; FDIC deposit insurance guidance for liquidity distinctions. This is educational material, not financial, legal, underwriting or tax advice.</p>\n</section></div><h3>Primary sources</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/save-and-invest/figure-out-your-finances\">SEC Investor.gov · Figure Out Your Finances</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/financial-well-being/about/\">CFPB · Why financial well-being?</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/loan-estimate/\">CFPB · Loan Estimate explainer</a></li></ul>","L1.S2":"<div class=\"eyebrow\">Academic Edition 3 · Candidate</div><h1>L1.S2 — Cash Flow vs. Net Worth</h1><div class=\"notice\">Additive textbook supplement. Your live app, progress, bookmarks and legacy assessment positions have not been changed.</div><div class=\"intro\"><h1>L1.S2 — Cash Flow vs. Net Worth</h1>\n<p><strong>Academic reconstruction · Supplement to Gold Edition 2 · Candidate Edition 3</strong><br />\n<strong>Discipline:</strong> Household cash-flow accounting, financial forecasting and operations.<br />\n<strong>Prerequisite:</strong> L1.S1 (dated assets, liabilities, wealth, cash versus borrowing). Understand percentages and signed numbers.<br />\n<strong>Outcomes:</strong> Prepare a cash-flow reconciliation; distinguish economic expense from financing and asset conversion; forecast daily minimum cash; model 26-paycheck/12-month mismatch; build a sinking-fund schedule and three-month liquidity stress test; evaluate feasible interventions and explain reporting limitations.</p>\n</div><div class=\"lab\" data-lab=\"L1.S2\"><h3>Try It · Mathematical learning lab</h3><p>Local demonstration only; no real financial data, no saving, and no credit or investment guarantee.</p><div class=\"controls\"><label>Starting checking ($)<input data-v=\"opening\" type=\"number\" value=\"900\"></label><label>Rent before payroll ($)<input data-v=\"rent\" type=\"number\" value=\"1200\" min=\"0\"></label><label>Paycheck after rent ($)<input data-v=\"pay\" type=\"number\" value=\"1400\" min=\"0\"></label><label>Later bills ($)<input data-v=\"bills\" type=\"number\" value=\"500\" min=\"0\"></label></div><div class=\"results\"><div class=\"metric\"><span>Checking after rent</span><b data-out=\"floor\"></b></div><div class=\"metric\"><span>Minimum extra opening cash</span><b data-out=\"needed\"></b></div><div class=\"metric\"><span>Ending cash</span><b data-out=\"end\"></b></div></div></div><div class=\"academic\"><section id=\"L1.S2.A1\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S2.A1</div><h2>A. Financial stocks, flows and operating capacity</h2>\n<p>A bank account balance is a <strong>stock</strong> measured at an instant. Net earnings and expenses are <strong>flows</strong> over a period. A net-worth change is not necessarily equal to a change in bank cash because the household may borrow, repay principal, buy assets, receive noncash employer contributions, or see asset prices move. A household that appears 'profitable' by an informal budget can still run out of cash on the fifth of the month. Conversely, receiving a $5,000 loan may end a short-term shortage while increasing future obligations without making the household wealthier.</p>\n<p>For teaching, build three separate statements: (1) a dated statement of financial position; (2) an income-and-expense statement for the period, with explicit treatment of noncash changes and economic expenses; (3) a cash movement statement that reconciles beginning and ending cash. Unlike formal GAAP accounting for publicly traded companies, this is a clearly labeled <strong>managerial household reporting framework</strong>, not a claim that families must use corporate financial-reporting rules.</p>\n<p>The fundamental cash identity is (C_{t}=C_{t-1}+\\sum\\text{cash inflows}_t-\\sum\\text{cash outflows}_t). Internal transfers among accounts included in total C offset and should not appear as external income or spending. The measure can be adjusted to <strong>available cash</strong>, excluding funds already committed to earlier obligations and recognizing holds or pending transactions. The CFPB's <em>Your Money, Your Goals</em> toolkit explicitly teaches starting balance, receipts, expenses and carry-forward balances over weeks to identify timing shortages (https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/educator-tool/your-money-your-goals/toolkit/; accessed 2026-11-01).</p>\n</section><section id=\"L1.S2.A2\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S2.A2</div><h2>B. The classified cash-flow statement</h2>\n<p>Begin with a fictional household's 30-day checking and savings transactions. Opening external bank cash is $1,800. Net wages credited = $4,200; a refund on a previously purchased household good = $100; proceeds of a new loan = $1,500. The bank shows rent $1,300, groceries $600, utilities $250, transport and repairs $500, loan <strong>principal</strong> $300, loan <strong>interest</strong> $45, cash saved to an external brokerage investment $250, and an internal transfer of $400 from checking to savings. The transfer has equal and opposite entries within combined bank cash: it nets to zero.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"text-align:left\">Cash flow classification</th>\n<th style=\"text-align:right\">Amount</th>\n<th style=\"text-align:left\">Economic interpretation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Opening combined bank cash</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$1,800</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Point-in-time asset</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Earned net wages</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">+$4,200</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Operating cash receipt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Refund</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">+$100</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Reverses part of prior spending; not earned pay</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Loan received</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">+$1,500</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Financing receipt, creates equal debt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Rent, food, utilities, transport/repair</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">−$2,650</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Consumption/operating payments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Principal repaid</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">−$300</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Financing cash, reduces debt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Loan interest</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">−$45</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Financing cost/economic expense</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Brokerage contribution</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">−$250</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Cash becomes a financial asset, not consumption</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Internal checking–savings transfer</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$0</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Reclassification within cash boundary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\"><strong>Closing combined bank cash</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>$4,355</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">1,800+4,200+100+1,500−2,650−300−45−250</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The <strong>change in total bank cash</strong> is +$2,555, but this is not an increase in net worth of $2,555. The loan added $1,500 in matching liabilities; the $250 brokerage contribution exchanges bank cash for another asset. The principal repayment changes both assets and liabilities. A simple net-worth bridge (ignoring asset-price changes and any tax timing) is +$4,200 wages +$100 refund −$2,650 consumption −$45 interest = <strong>+$1,605</strong>. You should reconcile this with the balance sheet: change in bank assets $2,555, change in brokerage investments +$250 and change in debt +$1,200 (1,500 proceeds −300 principal) means (\\Delta W=2,555+250−1,200=$1,605). This is the difference between <strong>account movements</strong> and economic outcomes.</p>\n<p>The classification demands evidence. If the $100 is a return of cash for an item initially purchased during a previous period, how it appears in the income-and-expense report depends on the reporting convention; it should never be casually mislabeled wages. If the brokerage transfer were instead a transfer between two included <em>cash</em> accounts, it would net to zero. Define the consolidation boundary once. Reconciliation failures often arise because one reporting tool includes a brokerage cash sweep but another does not.</p>\n</section><section id=\"L1.S2.A3\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S2.A3</div><h2>C. From monthly sums to dated solvency</h2>\n<p>A monthly budget of $2,800 wages and $2,950 expenses suggests a manageable $150 shortfall, but says nothing about <strong>when</strong> the shortfall occurs. Consider the daily ledger below. The starting cash is $900; a $1,200 rent payment is due on day 5, before the first $1,400 paycheck on day 9. A payment may be rejected or cause a fee; negative modeled balance is a <strong>funding gap</strong>, not a promise that the bank extends free credit.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"text-align:right\">Day</th>\n<th style=\"text-align:left\">Cash event</th>\n<th style=\"text-align:right\">Change</th>\n<th style=\"text-align:right\">Modeled bank cash</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">1</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Opening</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">—</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$900</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">5</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Rent</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">−$1,200</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>−$300</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">9</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Paycheck</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">+$1,400</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$1,100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">10</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Groceries</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">−$300</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$800</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">17</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Insurance</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">−$200</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$600</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">23</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Paycheck</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">+$1,400</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$2,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">28</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Utilities</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">−$250</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$1,750</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">30</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Other obligations</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">−$1,000</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\"><strong>$750</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The month ends with $750, but the minimum modeled cash was <strong>−$300 on day 5</strong>. For a fixed event set, the <em>minimum opening cushion</em> necessary to avoid going below zero equals (\\max(0,-\\min_t C_t)); here the extra opening cushion is $300. If an employer instead pays a day earlier or the rent can lawfully be paid on a different date without penalty, a change in timing may solve the minimum-cash problem with no change to total monthly net income. However, an apparent timing fix might merely push the shortage into next month. Always carry closing cash forward and project at least another cycle.</p>\n<p>The formula assumes events and posting dates known with certainty. In reality, an automatic debit might settle overnight, a paycheck might post at a different hour, and pending card authorizations can reduce available balance. Model a <strong>worst-case posting order</strong> when several events fall on the same date; verify bank terms before promising any payment will settle. A trustworthy forecast marks projected versus posted amounts and never silently treats a projected paycheck as received cash.</p>\n</section><section id=\"L1.S2.A4\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S2.A4</div><h2>D. The calendar mismatch: 26 biweekly checks, 12 rent payments</h2>\n<p>A biweekly worker paid $1,400 every 14 days generally expects 26 payments in 364 days: ($1,400×26=$36,400) across a standard 26-check year, or a <strong>planning average</strong> of $3,033.33 per calendar month. Yet 26/12 is 2.1667, not two checks exactly each month. Many months receive two checks ($2,800), a few receive three, and an unusual 27-paycheck calendar can occur depending on payroll dates. Never spend the expected 'extra' paycheck before it actually arrives.</p>\n<p>Suppose monthly rent is $1,200. A naive rent contribution of $600 from every check works in a two-paycheck month if nothing else interferes, but allocating the entire yearly rent by paycheck produces ($14,400/26=$553.85) per check, on average. The $553.85 figure is useful for <strong>long-run sinking-fund contribution planning</strong>, not a guarantee that this month's rent is funded: an October 30 check cannot pay an October 10 rent deadline. The exact pay calendar, starting reserve and already-funded portion of upcoming rent determine the true necessary set-aside. A payroll-first model works backward from the next obligation date and asks how much must be retained from checks before it.</p>\n<p>For a fixed $1,200 rent, a worker who begins with no dedicated rent reserve and receives only one remaining check before rent must earmark the full $1,200 from that check, not $553.85. Once an adequate cycle reserve has been built, the household can use the average contribution as a maintenance rule and still run the calendar check. Failure to distinguish <em>annual averaging</em> from <em>bill-by-bill liquidity</em> is a frequent source of late fees despite positive annual income.</p>\n</section><section id=\"L1.S2.A5\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S2.A5</div><h2>E. Sinking funds and the opportunity cost of cash</h2>\n<p>A sinking fund is money accumulated for a predictable but nonmonthly obligation. If a $1,080 insurance premium is due in eight months and $320 is already set aside, the funding gap is $760; the required equal monthly deposits are $760/8 = <strong>$95</strong>, assuming no interest and exact deposits. Do not divide the total $1,080 by eight, which double-counts existing reserves, and do not label a future premium 'unexpected' when its payment date is known.</p>\n<p>A household may choose between placing short-horizon cash in a liquid insured savings account and investing it in a volatile security. The decision depends on the <strong>time horizon and loss tolerance</strong>, not simply which option has the highest average historical return. A 10% asset decline on the month when car insurance is due may create forced-sale losses. The opportunity cost of holding safe cash is the return forgone elsewhere; the benefit is preserving the ability to pay a known obligation. Near-term cash decisions should be evaluated on after-tax, after-fee liquidity, predictable access, deposit insurance where relevant and the consequences of a shortfall.</p>\n<p>The FDIC currently describes standard insurance coverage as $250,000 per depositor per FDIC-insured bank per ownership category, subject to ownership rules (https://www.fdic.gov/resources/deposit-insurance/faq; accessed 2026-11-01). This does not mean a mutual fund held in a bank brokerage is FDIC-insured; type of product and ownership category matter. We cover these legal protections in depth in later investing modules, but even a beginner's cash reserve plan needs to distinguish insured deposits from investment securities.</p>\n</section><section id=\"L1.S2.A6\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S2.A6</div><h2>F. Forecasting error and stress testing</h2>\n<p>Professional cash forecasting contains at least a base case, a timing shock, and an income/expense shock. In a three-month household model, start with $2,000 accessible cash, $3,400 monthly net wages, and $3,150 monthly recurring cash outflows. Base case: month-end cash after months 1, 2 and 3 is $2,250, $2,500 and $2,750. If month 2 additionally has a $1,200 car repair, balances become $2,250, <strong>$1,300</strong>, $1,550. If income is also reduced by $700 in month 3, the final balance is <strong>$850</strong>. A single $250 'monthly surplus' hides important tail events.</p>\n<p>These simplified month-end figures do <strong>not</strong> establish that every interim obligation is covered: the actual daily floor might be below zero. A complete model first reconciles opening and closing balances, then identifies the minimum daily cash, then stress tests the timing of receipts and payments. The order is important. An elegant forecast chart can be perfectly arithmetic and still fail as a payment plan if its time resolution is too coarse.</p>\n<p>Use a scenario table recording named assumptions; do not encode 'bad times' as a mysterious 20% haircut without naming which income source or cost changes. For a three-month horizon, calculate (i) a 10-day delayed paycheck, (ii) a one-time repair, (iii) 15% higher utility bills, and (iv) a combined case. A defensible recommendation may be to postpone a discretionary $250 investment contribution or arrange a no-fee change in billing schedule where available, not to take a costly emergency loan by default. Some situations have no feasible solution without external assistance or negotiated relief; show that explicitly rather than hiding the negative balance.</p>\n<h3>Independent homework — solve before opening the key</h3>\n<p><strong>P1 — Reconciliation (20 points).</strong> Opening combined bank cash $2,200; wages $3,800; refund $60; proceeds of new loan $1,000; rent $1,250; groceries $520; utilities $225; principal repayment $280; interest $35; brokerage investment $300; checking-to-savings transfer $700. Compute closing combined bank cash, external cash change, total debt change and modeled change in net worth. State which cash entries are not economic income or expense.</p>\n<p><strong>P2 — Cash-calendar floor (20 points).</strong> Start day 1 with $1,100. On day 3 rent $1,400, day 9 paycheck $1,450, day 13 auto $350, day 21 paycheck $1,450, day 24 utilities $240, day 30 groceries $500. Determine cash after every event, minimum modeled balance and smallest <strong>additional</strong> opening reserve required to keep cash nonnegative. Is closing positive?</p>\n<p><strong>P3 — Paycheck allocation (15 points).</strong> An illustrative worker receives $1,350 biweekly. Calculate 26-check income, monthly planning average, average yearly rent set-aside per check for rent $1,350 monthly. If only one paycheck remains before rent and the rent fund contains $450, how much must be reserved from that paycheck to avoid a rent-fund shortfall? Explain why these numbers differ.</p>\n<p><strong>P4 — Sinking fund (10 points).</strong> A $1,420 known property charge is due in nine months. $340 is already saved. Without interest, calculate equal monthly funding. Recalculate if a revised bill is $1,600 and only eight deposits remain, with $460 accumulated at that time.</p>\n<p><strong>P5 — Three-period scenario (15 points).</strong> Start $1,750, monthly wages $3,200, recurring expenses $3,050. In month two a $900 repair occurs; month three wages fall by $600. Compute three month-end balances under the base and combined downside cases and identify the missing information needed to determine if a payment will be late.</p>\n<p><strong>P6 — Sources and independent recommendation (20 points).</strong> Use the CFPB cash-flow toolkit. Show the opening balance, income and expenses of one illustrative week, compute its closing balance, and explain a way the weekly balance can disagree with the month-end average. In 300–500 words, evaluate whether a household should make a $300 brokerage contribution while it has an uncovered $250 bill due tomorrow. Distinguish mechanical affordability, long-run investment opportunity cost and ethical judgment.</p>\n<details><summary>Show worked solutions &amp; answer explanations</summary><h3>Answer key and grading</h3>\n<p><strong>P1.</strong> External inflow 3,800+60+1,000=$4,860; external outflow 1,250+520+225+280+35+300=$2,610; closing =2,200+4,860−2,610=$4,450. Cash change +$2,250. Debt +$720 (=1,000−280). Brokerage asset +$300. Net worth change =2,250+300−720=+$1,830; equivalently wages+refund−rent−groceries−utilities−interest=3,800+60−1,250−520−225−35=1,830. Loan receipt is not earnings; principal repayment is not an economic expense; investment transfer is asset conversion; intra-bank $700 transfer nets to zero.</p>\n<p><strong>P2.</strong> Balances: opening 1,100; after rent <strong>−300</strong>; after pay 1,150; after auto 800; after second pay 2,250; after utilities 2,010; after groceries <strong>1,510</strong>. Floor −$300; additional opening buffer $300. Month ends positive but has a rent-date shortage.</p>\n<p><strong>P3.</strong> Annual =1,350×26=$35,100; monthly average $2,925; annual rent 16,200; contribution average =16,200/26≈$623.08 per check. Next bill still needs 1,350−450=$900 from the single check (if sufficient other cash exists). $623.08 is annual smoothing, $900 is actual payment-deadline funding.</p>\n<p><strong>P4.</strong> (1,420−340)/9=$120 per month. Revised = (1,600−460)/8=$142.50 per month.</p>\n<p><strong>P5.</strong> Base closes $1,900, $2,050, $2,200. Downside $1,900; month 2 $1,900+3,200−3,050−900=$1,150; month 3 $1,150+(3,200−600)−3,050=$700. Missing: actual dates for income, rent and other debits; holds; available versus ledger cash; account boundaries.</p>\n<p><strong>P6.</strong> Grading requires an authentic CFPB tool citation, a correct weekly opening-plus-inflows-minus-outflows schedule (8 points), clear explanation of timing (4), and a defensible recommendation with assumptions and downside risks (8). No single mandated investment recommendation. A $300 investment while $250 cannot be paid tomorrow is mechanically infeasible absent other uncommitted cash or a feasible payment schedule adjustment; an expected future return is not cash available today.</p>\n</details><p><strong>Mastery gate:</strong> ≥80/100 overall; ≥70% on numerical P1–P5; satisfactory sourced judgment on P6. Keep all original Gold Edition 2 questions unchanged; grade this open-response supplement separately. Never treat a numerical lab as a bank transaction or connect it to real accounts.</p>\n<p><strong>Sources checked 2026-11-01:</strong> CFPB <em>Your Money, Your Goals</em> budgeting tools, CFPB cash-flow guidance, FDIC deposit insurance information, SEC Investor.gov personal-finance statement guidance. Dates and figures in the cases are illustrative, not current offers or personally inferred values.</p>\n</section></div><h3>Primary sources</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/educator-tool/your-money-your-goals/toolkit/\">CFPB · Your Money, Your Goals</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.fdic.gov/resources/deposit-insurance/faq\">FDIC · Deposit insurance FAQs</a></li></ul>","L1.S3":"<div class=\"eyebrow\">Academic Edition 3 · Candidate</div><h1>L1.S3 — Compound Growth</h1><div class=\"notice\">Additive textbook supplement. Your live app, progress, bookmarks and legacy assessment positions have not been changed.</div><div class=\"intro\"><h1>L1.S3 — Compound Growth</h1>\n<p><strong>Academic reconstruction · Supplement to Gold Edition 2 · Candidate Edition 3</strong><br />\n<strong>Discipline:</strong> Financial mathematics and time-value decision analysis.<br />\n<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> L1.S1 and L1.S2, exponents, percent-to-decimal conversion, and basic algebra.<br />\n<strong>Outcomes:</strong> Derive single-sum and annuity growth formulas; distinguish nominal APR, effective annual yield and inflation-adjusted growth; discount future amounts; reconcile contribution timing; construct nominal/real and fee-sensitive projections; solve inverse savings targets; evaluate NPV and alternative uses of cash; explain return-sequence and risk limitations.</p>\n</div><div class=\"lab\" data-lab=\"L1.S3\"><h3>Try It · Mathematical learning lab</h3><p>Local demonstration only; no real financial data, no saving, and no credit or investment guarantee.</p><div class=\"controls\"><label>Monthly end deposit ($)<input data-v=\"deposit\" type=\"number\" value=\"250\" min=\"0\"></label><label>Nominal annual return (%)<input data-v=\"rate\" type=\"number\" value=\"6\" step=\"0.1\"></label><label>Years<input data-v=\"years\" type=\"number\" value=\"20\" min=\"1\" max=\"60\"></label><label>Annual inflation assumption (%)<input data-v=\"inflation\" type=\"number\" value=\"3\" step=\"0.1\"></label></div><div class=\"results\"><div class=\"metric\"><span>Illustrative future value</span><b data-out=\"future\"></b></div><div class=\"metric\"><span>Your contributions</span><b data-out=\"contrib\"></b></div><div class=\"metric\"><span>Illustrative growth</span><b data-out=\"growth\"></b></div><div class=\"metric\"><span>Present-day purchasing power</span><b data-out=\"real\"></b></div></div></div><div class=\"academic\"><section id=\"L1.S3.A1\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S3.A1</div><h2>A. Why time has an economic price</h2>\n<p>A dollar today can be consumed now, held as reserves or invested for later. A dollar promised later cannot pay today's rent; whether the promise is worth as much as a dollar today depends on its timing, credit risk, inflation, flexibility, liquidity and alternative uses. This leads to the <strong>time value of money</strong>: cash flows at different dates must be brought to a common date before they are compared. A discount factor is a method, not a guarantee that a projected payoff will arrive.</p>\n<p>The intuitive foundation of compounding is <strong>growth applied to a changing base</strong>. If an account starts with $1,000 and earns an effective 5% per year with all interest retained, one year ends at $1,050; the second year adds 5% of $1,050 ($52.50), not merely another $50, so the second ending value is $1,102.50. A linear model would give $1,100 after two years and increasingly understate growth at positive rates. At negative investment returns, compounding can shrink the base; the process is mathematically symmetric in direction but investment losses have other behavioral and cash-flow consequences.</p>\n<p>The SEC's Investor.gov compound-interest calculator allows a starting amount, contribution, period, rate and compounding frequency (https://www.investor.gov/financial-tools-calculators/calculators/compound-interest-calculator; checked 2026-11-01). It is a projection tool; it does not establish a predictable return for stocks or funds. A stock portfolio may rise 20%, then fall 30%, and may not be worth what a smooth 6% line suggests at a home down-payment date.</p>\n</section><section id=\"L1.S3.A2\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S3.A2</div><h2>B. Derive the single-sum formulas</h2>\n<p>Let (P) be an initial cash amount, (i) the <strong>effective growth rate per period</strong>, (n) the number of equally long periods, and (F) the balance after n periods. One period: (F_1=P+Pi=P(1+i)). A second period multiplies the first result by ((1+i)): (F_2=P(1+i)^2). Repeating n periods yields:</p>\n<p>[F=P(1+i)^n.]</p>\n<p>Solve for present value by dividing by the positive compound factor:</p>\n<p>[P=\\frac{F}{(1+i)^n}.]</p>\n<p>This reverse operation is called <strong>discounting</strong>. For a certain $5,000 payment in five years discounted at a hypothetical 5% effective annual rate, present value is $5,000/1.05^5 = approximately <strong>$3,917.63</strong>. The rate is a judgment about appropriate opportunity cost and risk. Discounting an unsecured, uncertain IOU at the same rate as a guaranteed insured deposit is usually not justified merely because the two figures share a payment date.</p>\n<p>Algebraic variants let us solve for time (n=\\ln(F/P)/\\ln(1+i)) (provided P,F positive and i not zero) or for the implied rate (i=(F/P)^{1/n}-1). If i=0, the present and future values are equal. If i approaches −100%, the compound factor tends toward zero for positive n; standard formulas should reject i≤−1 for ordinary account growth. An account that loses 50% must subsequently gain 100% to recover its original value; this follows because 0.5×2=1, not because of a special financial rule.</p>\n</section><section id=\"L1.S3.A3\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S3.A3</div><h2>C. Derive the contribution-annuity formula</h2>\n<p>Many households invest a fixed amount every month instead of a single deposit. For a contribution (c) made <strong>at the end of each period</strong>, immediately after the period's investment return is applied, the latest payment earns no interest by the measurement date. The earlier deposits accumulate according to their time outstanding:</p>\n<p>[F_n=c\\big[(1+i)^{n-1}+(1+i)^{n-2}+\\cdots+(1+i)^1+1\\big].]</p>\n<p>To sum the geometric series, let (S=1+(1+i)+\\cdots+(1+i)^{n-1}). Multiply by ((1+i)), subtract S and cancel interior terms: (iS=(1+i)^n-1). Hence (S=[(1+i)^n-1]/i) for i≠0. Therefore:</p>\n<p>[F_n=c\\frac{(1+i)^n-1}{i}, \\quad i\\ne0.]</p>\n<p>At i=0, the correct limiting value is (F_n=cn) because there is no growth. A contribution at the <strong>beginning</strong> of each period instead earns one additional period and produces (F_n^{due}=F_n(1+i)). These two schedules must never be presented as equivalent. For an initial principal P plus end-of-month contributions c, add (P(1+i)^n) to the annuity term. Use whole-number n for exact monthly schedules; a formula with n=12×years assumes months and deposit dates align with that definition.</p>\n<p>With $250 deposited each month for 20 years, 6% <strong>nominal annual rate compounded monthly</strong> means (i=.06/12=.005), (n=240), and:\\n\n[F=250\\frac{(1.005)^{240}-1}{.005}=$115,510.22\\text{ approximately}.]</p>\n<p>Total contributions are $250×240=$60,000; the illustration's growth equals $55,510.22 before investment costs, taxes and inflation. The smooth rate is hypothetical and is not a return forecast; the periodic-contribution convention must be disclosed. At 0%, the answer is $60,000; at hypothetical 3% it is $82,075.50; and at 9% it is $166,971.72. This is <strong>sensitivity</strong>, not probability: those rates have not been assigned likelihoods.</p>\n</section><section id=\"L1.S3.A4\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S3.A4</div><h2>D. Nominal rates, APY and inflation</h2>\n<p>A nominal annual interest rate with m equally spaced compounding periods per year converts to the effective annual yield (APY=(1+j/m)^m-1). Conversely, if APY is known and contributions compound monthly, the consistent monthly effective rate is (i=(1+APY)^{1/12}-1), <strong>not</strong> APY/12. These formulas matter for deposits because 6% quoted with monthly compounding is an effective annual rate of about 6.168%, whereas 6% APY corresponds to a slightly smaller nominal monthly rate. Investment returns are generally uncertain; treating historical average returns like bank APY is a modeling simplification that must be labeled.</p>\n<p>Inflation changes the measuring unit. Suppose a portfolio grows by 6% while the general price level rises 3% in the same year. One dollar of initial purchasing power turns into nominal $1.06, but each comparable basket now costs $1.03. The real growth factor is (1.06/1.03); exact real return is:</p>\n<p>[r_{real}=\\frac{1+r_{nominal}}{1+\\pi}-1=2.9126%.]</p>\n<p>Simply subtracting rates (6−3=3%) is an approximation, generally adequate only when rates are small and precision is not important. For multiple years with constant assumptions, express terminal future dollars at starting-date purchasing power as (F_{real}=F_{nominal}/(1+\\pi)^n). Do not mix a nominal discount rate with inflation-adjusted real cash flows; either compare nominal cash flows with a compatible nominal rate or real flows with a compatible real rate.</p>\n<p>For research, the BLS CPI calculator uses an unadjusted CPI-U urban-consumer price index to compare general purchasing power across dates (https://www.bls.gov/data/inflation_calculator.htm; checked 2026-11-01). A particular Connecticut household's housing, energy and insurance costs can change differently from the nationwide CPI. CPI answers a defined price-index question, not a personalized forecast of expenses. Students should identify whether their dataset is monthly, annual, seasonally adjusted or not before computing a rate.</p>\n</section><section id=\"L1.S3.A5\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S3.A5</div><h2>E. Fees, taxes, withdrawals and the liquidity constraint</h2>\n<p>Compounding is sensitive not just to the headline return but to account fees and actual contribution timing. Suppose the 20-year monthly deposit model assumes 6% gross nominal return and an illustrative one-percentage-point annual drag, approximated as a <strong>5% net nominal return compounded monthly</strong>. The ending amount changes from about $115,510.22 to <strong>$102,758.42</strong>, a difference of <strong>$12,751.81</strong>. In the real world, a percentage expense ratio is typically charged against fluctuating assets and net return is not necessarily gross return minus the displayed fee in each monthly period; this is a transparent teaching approximation.</p>\n<p>Taxes also differ by vehicle and timing. A bank may report taxable interest annually even if it stays deposited. A taxable brokerage may incur dividend tax or capital-gains tax upon realization, with exceptions and legal nuance. Qualified retirement accounts have different contribution, distribution and tax rules. Rather than applying an invented universal '15% investment tax', build after-tax cash-flow schedules from the actual account's rules in later taxation modules. A result before tax and fees must be labeled as such.</p>\n<p><strong>Liquidity</strong> is particularly important when investing for a home. A household planning to buy in 24 months should not assume that a volatile portfolio will retain its value precisely when a down payment becomes due. If $350 per month is saved for 24 end-of-month deposits in a hypothetical account earning <strong>3.5% APY</strong>, (i=(1.035)^{1/12}-1) and (F≈$8,683.26). Contributions are $8,400; the illustrative additional amount is about $283.26. Actual savings products vary, and the interest rate may reset; the example is not a rate quote. A money-market fund and a bank savings account can have different safety protections and risks.</p>\n</section><section id=\"L1.S3.A6\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S3.A6</div><h2>F. Opportunity cost, NPV and a first professional decision</h2>\n<p>The same dollar cannot be simultaneously allocated to a house deposit, an emergency fund, a credit card payoff and a retirement investment. <strong>Opportunity cost</strong> is the value of the best alternative forgone, not merely a line item in a budget. If the household carries a card charging 24% APR while comparing an uncertain 6% projected investment return, paying the card's principal can avoid future contractual interest when it reduces interest-bearing balance. But the relative rates are not the whole decision: the household must protect essential liquidity and consider grace periods, tax rules, fees and how the card calculates interest. The contract rate and volatile market return are not equivalent kinds of guarantees.</p>\n<p><strong>Net present value (NPV)</strong> translates a series of dated expected cash flows to a common present date and subtracts the initial outlay. For equally spaced period-end cash flows, (NPV=-C_0+\\sum_{t=1}^n CF_t/(1+r)^t). Consider an invented $3,000 expenditure today expected to produce $1,200 at the ends of years 1, 2 and 3 with an 8% annual discount rate. The three present values are approximately $1,111.11, $1,028.81 and $952.60; NPV ≈ <strong>+$92.52</strong>. This is a small positive margin on the assumed discount rate, not proof the actual outcome is attractive. Delays, tax, risk, time demands and measurement error could reverse it.</p>\n<p>A <strong>decision memo</strong> should disclose the initial cost, expected cash-flow dates, risk sources, discount-rate rationale, scenario sensitivity and an explicit alternative. A household does not own an arbitrage simply because a projected investment percentage exceeds a borrowing APR. Borrowing creates a payment obligation even if the asset declines. Later modules expand this basic NPV method into investment analysis, bond pricing and corporate capital budgeting.</p>\n<h3>Independent homework — solve before opening key</h3>\n<p><strong>P1 — Formula derivation (15 points).</strong> Derive the end-period annuity formula from the geometric series and explain its i→0 limit. Show how beginning-period contributions alter the answer. Identify which date earns the first period of growth.</p>\n<p><strong>P2 — Nominal versus effective (15 points).</strong> Calculate APY for a nominal 6% annual interest rate compounded monthly. Calculate the monthly effective rate corresponding to 6% APY. State why the two are not exactly the same model.</p>\n<p><strong>P3 — Savings schedule (20 points).</strong> Starting at $0, contribute $300 at the end of each month for 10 years at a hypothetical nominal 4.8% compounded monthly. Calculate total contributed, ending balance, modeled growth and the no-interest limit. Recompute if the $300 contributions are made at each month's beginning.</p>\n<p><strong>P4 — Purchasing power (15 points).</strong> An account grows 7% nominal during a year in which a matched price index rises 4%. Compute exact real return. A hypothetical $50,000 becomes $80,000 after five years; if inflation was a uniform 3% annually, express the $80,000 in starting-date dollars. Explain the two different questions.</p>\n<p><strong>P5 — Goal sensitivity (10 points).</strong> A two-year home fund receives $350 at the end of each month. Compute future value at 0% and at 3.5% APY. Explain why this exact computation alone does not establish the probability of having enough cash at closing.</p>\n<p><strong>P6 — Discounting and choice (15 points).</strong> A fictional project costs $3,000 today and pays $1,200 at each of three year-ends. Compute NPV at 8% and 12%. State your conclusion and the condition under which it would change.</p>\n<p><strong>P7 — Source and judgment (10 points).</strong> Using SEC Investor.gov's calculator and BLS CPI guidance, identify the frequency/return and price-index assumptions you would need to make an honest twenty-year forecast. Write 200–350 words explaining why a smooth growth chart must not be represented as a guarantee or confused with near-term accessible funds.</p>\n<details><summary>Show worked solutions &amp; answer explanations</summary><h3>Answer key and scoring rationale</h3>\n<p><strong>P1.</strong> (S=1+(1+i)+\\cdots+(1+i)^{n-1}); ((1+i)S-S=(1+i)^n-1), therefore (S=[(1+i)^n-1]/i). (F=cS). For i→0, each term approaches 1, so F→cn. Beginning-period deposits multiply by (1+i). End-of-month first deposit earns growth for the remaining n−1 periods, last earns zero by the end date.</p>\n<p><strong>P2.</strong> ((1+.06/12)^{12}-1≈6.1678%). Monthly effective rate corresponding to 6% APY = ((1.06)^{1/12}-1≈0.48676%), not exactly 0.5%. State annual compounding conventions, otherwise the numeric answer is incomplete.</p>\n<p><strong>P3.</strong> (i=.048/12=.004), n=120. Contributions = $36,000. (F=300[(1.004)^{120}-1]/.004) (calculate to cents using model). Numerically F≈<strong>$46,089.59</strong>, growth≈<strong>$10,089.59</strong>; no-interest $36,000. Beginning-period F≈<strong>$46,273.95</strong> (=end-period F×1.004). A correct solution must show the n and timing convention, not a rounded 'annual average'.</p>\n<p><strong>P4.</strong> Exact real =1.07/1.04−1=2.8846%. Starting-date spending power =$80,000/(1.03)^5≈$69,008.70. First asks a one-year percentage, second asks a five-year inflation-adjusted level.</p>\n<p><strong>P5.</strong> No growth $8,400; 3.5%-APY future amount ≈$8,683.26, with 24 end-of-month payments. Rates might change, accounts have different protections and inflation can affect actual purchasing power.</p>\n<p><strong>P6.</strong> 8% NPV≈+$92.52; 12% NPV≈<strong>−$117.80</strong> and is negative (so project fails that discount hurdle under stated cash flows). Decision can reverse if timing, costs, cash flows or the appropriate discount rate change. Include a discussion of the project's risk and household liquidity.</p>\n<p><strong>P7.</strong> Requires primary source links, at least four specifically named assumptions (initial investment, recurring deposit schedule, annual return/compounding, inflation series/date, plus optionally tax/fees), and a clear statement that random market returns and liquidity access differ from smooth hypothetical interest calculations.</p>\n</details><p><strong>Mastery gate:</strong> ≥80 overall; correct derivation and timing on P1; ≥70% on numerical work; defensible P7 explanation. All money illustrations are pre-tax, pre-fee unless specifically stated. No investment return forecast, insurance recommendation or financial-product recommendation is implied by these examples.</p>\n</section></div><h3>Primary sources</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.investor.gov/financial-tools-calculators/calculators/compound-interest-calculator\">SEC Investor.gov · Compound Interest Calculator</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.bls.gov/data/inflation_calculator.htm\">BLS · CPI Inflation Calculator</a></li></ul>","L1.S4":"<div class=\"eyebrow\">Academic Edition 3 · Candidate</div><h1>L1.S4 — Debt and Leverage</h1><div class=\"notice\">Additive textbook supplement. Your live app, progress, bookmarks and legacy assessment positions have not been changed.</div><div class=\"intro\"><h1>L1.S4 — Debt and Leverage</h1>\n<p><strong>Academic reconstruction · Supplement to Gold Edition 2 · Candidate Edition 3</strong><br />\n<strong>Discipline:</strong> Consumer credit mathematics, solvency, risk management, financial decision analysis.<br />\n<strong>Prerequisites:</strong> L1.S1 financial position, L1.S2 dated cash flow, L1.S3 compounding/discounting, exponents.<br />\n<strong>Outcomes:</strong> Classify secured and unsecured obligations; distinguish APR, payment, term, balance and borrowing cost; derive and independently verify an amortization schedule; compare interest-rate and term sensitivity; calculate debt-service and collateral ratios with clearly defined denominators; model high-rate revolving credit and promotional loans cautiously; assess leverage outcomes including loss of equity and forced-sale risk; defend an ethical recommendation.</p>\n</div><div class=\"lab\" data-lab=\"L1.S4\"><h3>Try It · Mathematical learning lab</h3><p>Local demonstration only; no real financial data, no saving, and no credit or investment guarantee.</p><div class=\"controls\"><label>Loan principal ($)<input data-v=\"principal\" type=\"number\" value=\"12000\" min=\"0\"></label><label>Nominal annual note rate (%)<input data-v=\"rate\" type=\"number\" value=\"12\" step=\"0.1\"></label><label>Months<input data-v=\"months\" type=\"number\" value=\"24\" min=\"1\" max=\"600\"></label></div><div class=\"results\"><div class=\"metric\"><span>Scheduled monthly payment</span><b data-out=\"payment\"></b></div><div class=\"metric\"><span>First-period interest</span><b data-out=\"firstint\"></b></div><div class=\"metric\"><span>First principal reduction</span><b data-out=\"firstprinc\"></b></div><div class=\"metric\"><span>Total nominal interest</span><b data-out=\"totalint\"></b></div></div></div><div class=\"academic\"><section id=\"L1.S4.A1\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S4.A1</div><h2>A. Borrowing changes timing, not the fundamental resource constraint</h2>\n<p>Debt can bring purchasing power forward in time. A buyer can occupy a home or operate a vehicle now rather than waiting to accumulate the entire price, but must allocate future cash flow to settle principal, interest and any contract fees. Money received from a loan raises available bank cash and creates a matching liability; borrowing alone does <strong>not</strong> create net worth. The lender's claim usually survives an unfavorable investment outcome and may be secured by specified property. That asymmetry explains both the usefulness and the danger of leverage.</p>\n<p>Debt instruments differ. A <strong>fixed-payment installment loan</strong> typically has a scheduled repayment term and a payment determined by its interest basis. A <strong>revolving credit card</strong> allows variable borrowing and payments, with interest often computed from daily balances if the balance is not eligible for an interest-free grace period. A <strong>mortgage</strong> usually gives a lender a lien against the property and can include escrow, mortgage insurance and other costs outside principal-and-interest. An <strong>insurance policy loan</strong>, margin loan or HELOC can have different collateral, rates, calls, lapse or contract rules. Do not treat a single generic 'debt calculator' as a substitute for the actual agreements.</p>\n<p>The CFPB distinguishes mortgage principal-and-interest from the total monthly housing payment, which may include taxes, insurance and mortgage insurance (https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/on-a-mortgage-whats-the-difference-between-my-principal-and-interest-payment-and-my-total-monthly-payment-en-1941/; checked 2026-11-01). If a household chooses a house based only on the low principal-and-interest figure, it can underestimate the cash it needs every month. The first step in credit analysis is therefore to identify exactly what the quoted rate and payment include and exclude.</p>\n</section><section id=\"L1.S4.A2\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S4.A2</div><h2>B. Derive fixed-rate amortization rather than memorizing a calculator</h2>\n<p>Let <strong>P</strong> be the amount financed at origination; <strong>i</strong> the interest rate per equal payment period under a simple periodic-interest model; <strong>n</strong> the number of payments; <strong>M</strong> the level payment at the <strong>end</strong> of every period. At each period, the opening balance earns interest (iB), a payment M is made, and the closing balance is (B_{t}=B_{t-1}(1+i)-M). Starting at (B_0=P), repeated substitution produces:</p>\n<p>[B_n=P(1+i)^n-M\\left[1+(1+i)+\\cdots+(1+i)^{n-1}\\right].]</p>\n<p>For a fully amortizing loan ending with B_n=0, use the geometric-series result from L1.S3:</p>\n<p>[0=P(1+i)^n-M\\frac{(1+i)^n-1}{i}.]</p>\n<p>Solving for M gives:</p>\n<p>[M=\\frac{Pi}{1-(1+i)^{-n}},\\quad i\\ne0.]</p>\n<p>When i=0 the valid limiting formula is (M=P/n). The schedule's interest for month t is (I_t=iB_{t-1}); principal paid is (Q_t=M-I_t); closing balance is (B_t=B_{t-1}-Q_t). Sum all principal paid to confirm it equals the opening debt, allowing cents-rounding at the final payment. Sum all scheduled payments less original principal to compute the model's nominal interest cost, <strong>not</strong> necessarily an all-in APR calculation.</p>\n<h3>Full first-period example</h3>\n<p>A fictional $12,000, 24-month loan uses a <strong>12% nominal annual interest rate divided by twelve</strong>, with no fees and no prepayment. Then i=0.01 and n=24. The monthly payment is approximately <strong>$564.88</strong>. Month 1 interest = $12,000×0.01=$120.00; principal reduction = $564.8817−$120=$444.8817; ending balance ≈$11,555.12. Month 2 interest is about $115.55, principal reduction about $449.33. Later payments devote progressively less to interest as the opening principal declines, provided the stated rate and payment are fixed.</p>\n<p>Total scheduled payments before rounding ≈$564.8817×24=$13,557.16, so modeled interest ≈<strong>$1,557.16</strong>. This schedule assumes periodic interest and level payment conventions; actual loan contracts may use daily accrual, payoff-day interest, origination fees or different compounding. If APR includes noninterest charges, substituting APR/12 directly into a contract-rate amortization schedule can be wrong. Keep the nominal note rate, APR, fees and cash-flow method separate rather than conflating them.</p>\n<p>A basic spreadsheet's columns are Period, Opening Balance, Interest, Payment, Principal and Closing Balance. The first row begins at t=1. Interest = Opening×i; Principal = Payment−Interest; Closing = Opening−Principal. Next row's opening equals the preceding closing. In the final row, cap payment to the outstanding balance plus accrued interest if cents-rounding otherwise creates a tiny negative principal. Verify that all rows reconcile; the visible schedule is as important as its headline monthly payment.</p>\n</section><section id=\"L1.S4.A3\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S4.A3</div><h2>C. Rate and term sensitivity</h2>\n<p>Hold the $12,000 initial principal and 24-month term constant while changing <strong>hypothetical nominal contract interest rates</strong>. At 0%, M=$500.00. At 6%, M≈$531.85. At 12%, M≈$564.88. At 18%, M≈$599.09. At 24%, M≈$634.45. Higher rates raise both the monthly cash commitment and nominal lifetime interest for fixed principal and maturity. This comparison is a controlled sensitivity analysis, not an assertion that those exact credit offers are currently available.</p>\n<p>Now consider term extension. Longer loans commonly lower the scheduled periodic payment at a fixed interest rate but keep interest accruing over more periods, raising total nominal interest unless other features differ. The household may find the lower immediate cash burden safer, but the longer payoff may delay ownership equity and can raise the risk of owing more than an asset can sell for. A lower payment is not synonymous with a lower total cost. When comparing two realistic offers, include fees and any prepayment penalties; a genuine alternative comparison should also compute what happens if the same monthly cash is applied to both debts.</p>\n<p>For a 30-year, fixed-payment mortgage principal of $237,500 at an <strong>illustrative 6.5% annual nominal note rate</strong>, periodic i=.065/12 and n=360. Principal-and-interest payment is approximately <strong>$1,501.16</strong>. At 5% the figure is ~$1,274.95, and at 8% it is ~$1,742.69. These values <strong>exclude</strong> taxes, homeowners insurance, possible mortgage insurance, association fees, maintenance and other ownership costs; they are not live mortgage-rate quotes or underwriting determinations. Recompute the scenario using a complete total-monthly-housing-budget in Module 2.</p>\n</section><section id=\"L1.S4.A4\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S4.A4</div><h2>D. Debt burden, collateral and cash resilience</h2>\n<p>Use precise definitions. <strong>Loan-to-value (LTV)</strong> for a particular collateralized loan is modeled as (B/V), with outstanding mortgage balance B and defensible market property value V. A $237,500 loan on a $250,000 house starts at 95% LTV. A 10% home-price decline with debt unchanged creates LTV=$237,500/$225,000≈105.56%; negative gross equity equals $225,000−$237,500=−$12,500. This borrower still owes according to the note even when collateral value drops. The mortgage may have recourse and other provisions determined by applicable law and contract; a simplified model cannot pronounce the legal consequences of default.</p>\n<p><strong>Debt-to-income (DTI)</strong> in lending is often computed from qualifying <strong>monthly debt obligations divided by qualifying gross monthly income</strong>, not take-home pay or net worth. The exact included obligations and eligibility rules vary by loan program and lender. An educator's household <strong>cash-obligation ratio</strong> can separately use total required debt payments divided by net household income, but must not be mislabeled a lender's DTI. For example $850 monthly required debt service / $3,400 take-home wages=25%; this is a net-pay cash burden, <strong>not</strong> a mortgage underwriting DTI. It reveals what fraction of paycheck cash is claimed by obligations, but says nothing by itself about food, dependents, maintenance or rent.</p>\n<p>The correct stress test converts percentages into dates and dollars. If a household has $3,400 net pay, $850 debt service and $2,450 nondebt essentials, there is a $100 apparent margin. A $400 loss of income for one month turns margin to −$300, and a $700 car repair makes the combined month deficit −$1,000. Holding only $500 emergency cash cannot cover the combined modeled shock without other changes. A superficially manageable 25% cash-obligation ratio is therefore not evidence that further borrowing is safe.</p>\n</section><section id=\"L1.S4.A5\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S4.A5</div><h2>E. Revolving credit, promotional offers and daily interest</h2>\n<p>Credit cards are not generally modeled as a fixed-payment annuity. Many issuers calculate interest using balances tracked daily, and separate balances (purchases, cash advances, balance transfers) can have different APRs. The CFPB notes that daily periodic rates commonly use APR divided by 365 or sometimes 360, depending on issuer, and that grace periods affect whether interest applies to new purchases (https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-a-daily-periodic-rate-on-a-credit-card-en-46/; https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/how-does-my-credit-card-company-calculate-the-amount-of-interest-i-owe-en-51/; checked 2026-11-01).</p>\n<p>An <strong>approximate simple daily-interest demonstration</strong>, not a statement simulation: with $2,000 principal kept constant over 30 days and a 24% APR using 365 days, interest ≈$2,000×(.24/365)×30=<strong>$39.45</strong>. With daily compounding and no payments or fees, a mathematical alternative is $2,000[(1+.24/365)^30−1], slightly higher. Real card balances change with transactions and payment application; the issuer's method and grace status control the actual bill. Label the two methods and do not promise a customer one universal result.</p>\n<p>A 0% promotional transfer may charge an immediate fee and revert to a higher rate after a deadline. Suppose $5,000 is transferred with a 3% fee financed into the promotional balance: the starting balance is $5,150. To clear it in 12 equal zero-interest payments requires $429.17 per month before any additional spending or fees. A household with only $250 available for repayment per month pays $3,000 over twelve months and still owes $2,150, even before considering interest that may apply after the promotion. 'Zero percent' does not make the debt disappear; a viable plan must be supported by an actual cash calendar.</p>\n</section><section id=\"L1.S4.A6\"><div class=\"part-label\">Academic section · L1.S4.A6</div><h2>F. Leverage is a distribution of gains, losses and obligations</h2>\n<p>Buying an asset with borrowed money magnifies outcomes measured <strong>relative to the owner's invested equity</strong>. Ignore fees, taxes and loan-interest for one step to isolate market-price exposure. A $100,000 asset financed with $80,000 borrowing starts with $20,000 equity. If market value rises 10% to $110,000 while debt remains $80,000, equity becomes $30,000: a 50% equity increase. If value falls 10% to $90,000, equity becomes $10,000: a 50% equity decline. A 20% price decline wipes out the initial equity. Financing interest, trading fees, taxes, maintenance and forced-sale costs can worsen results.</p>\n<p>Why do some households borrow anyway? Debt may finance a stable long-lived use, permit necessary transportation, spread a large cost over years or support business assets that produce income. The economic case must compare the debt's certain payment schedule with the usefulness and uncertainties of the asset. Borrowing to invest in a volatile security creates mismatch: the asset's future return is uncertain while payment or margin requirements may be contractual. In a policy loan, interest can compound separately from policy cash value and unpaid balances can threaten coverage under contract terms; 'borrow from yourself for free' is not a general financial truth. Later insurance modules examine surrender values, tax treatment, lapse and beneficiaries in detail.</p>\n<p>A prudent recommendation distinguishes <strong>capacity to borrow</strong> from <strong>capacity to repay</strong>. Even if an offer is legally available, evaluate income variability, total debt costs, emergency reserves, collateral exposure, loan term, ability to refinance, adverse rates and the consequence of a missed payment. Advertisements promoting riskless spread between borrowing costs and investment returns should be investigated; if downside is omitted, the apparent profit is not a defensible investment analysis.</p>\n<h3>Independent homework — do before answer key</h3>\n<p><strong>P1 — Derivation (20 points).</strong> From (B_t=B_{t-1}(1+i)-M), derive the fixed-payment amortization formula. Explain the i=0 case and how the final balance verifies the schedule.</p>\n<p><strong>P2 — Amortization (20 points).</strong> A fictional $10,000 loan runs for 36 months at a nominal 9% annual rate compounded monthly, with no fees. Compute payment, first month's interest, first month's principal, month-one closing balance, total modeled interest. Provide a six-row schedule and reconcile the sixth closing principal using the formula.</p>\n<p><strong>P3 — Housing sensitivity (15 points).</strong> For a $237,500 principal, compare 30-year monthly principal-and-interest at 5%, 6.5% and 8% note rates, assuming monthly periodic interest. Calculate dollars of monthly difference between the low and high scenarios. Name at least four costs absent from the results.</p>\n<p><strong>P4 — Leverage arithmetic (10 points).</strong> Asset purchase $120,000, debt $96,000. Calculate initial equity and changes in equity if asset value rises/falls 15% and borrowing remains unchanged. Give percentage equity return for each price scenario before borrowing costs. At what price decline is initial equity zero?</p>\n<p><strong>P5 — Timing and revolving credit (15 points).</strong> With a $3,600 credit-card balance held constant 28 days, an illustrative 22% APR, 365-day simple daily accrual and no grace period, compute approximate interest. Explain why this is not necessarily the issuer's actual charge. A second card offers 0% for 12 months with a 4% transfer fee on the same $3,600; what equal monthly payment clears the fee-financed balance if there is no promotional interest? If household can pay only $250/month, how much balance remains after 12 such payments?</p>\n<p><strong>P6 — Debt ratios and professional review (10 points).</strong> A household has $4,100 gross monthly income, $3,250 net monthly income, and $1,400/month of defined lender-counted payments. Compute the simple gross-income DTI <strong>under the supplied definition</strong> and separately calculate payments/net-pay ratio. Explain why they are not interchangeable and what loan-specific information is absent.</p>\n<p><strong>P7 — Written memo (10 points).</strong> Evaluate a claim that a household can borrow against a permanent life-insurance policy at 6% and earn an 'easy guaranteed spread' by investing in an asset expected to return 9%. Identify at least five distinct model risks, contract terms, taxes, liquidity or ethical disclosure questions; do not assume that such a policy or return exists.</p>\n<details><summary>Show worked solutions &amp; answer explanations</summary><h3>Answers and grading guide</h3>\n<p><strong>P1.</strong> (B_n=P(1+i)^n-M[(1+i)^n-1]/i). Setting B_n=0 yields (M=Pi/[1-(1+i)^{-n}]). At i=0, M=P/n. Recompute each closing balance from opening+interest−payment and independently verify against the closed form; explain rounding.</p>\n<p><strong>P2.</strong> Use (i=.09/12=.0075), n=36. (M=10,000×.0075/[1-(1.0075)^{-36}]); first interest $75; first principal M−75; month-one closing 10,000−(M−75); total interest 36M−10,000. Numerically, M≈<strong>$318.00</strong>, first principal≈<strong>$243.00</strong>, month-one balance≈<strong>$9,757.00</strong>, total interest≈<strong>$1,447.90</strong>. The independent verification file supplies all six first rows: months 1–6 end at approximately $9,757.00, $9,512.18, $9,265.53, $9,017.02, $8,766.65 and $8,514.40. Full credit requires the interest rate convention and a schedule reconciliation.</p>\n<p><strong>P3.</strong> 5%≈$1,274.95/month; 6.5%≈$1,501.16/month; 8%≈$1,742.69/month. High-low ≈$467.74/month. Missing: property taxes, homeowners insurance, mortgage insurance when applicable, maintenance/repairs, association fees and transaction/prepayment costs. Do not equate P&amp;I with full housing cost.</p>\n<p><strong>P4.</strong> Starting equity=$24,000. Up 15% gives $138,000 asset and $42,000 equity, +$18,000 equity or <strong>+75%</strong>. Down 15% gives $102,000 asset and $6,000 equity, −$18,000 or <strong>−75%</strong>. Price drop of 20% leaves value $96,000 and equity $0 before selling cost and interest.</p>\n<p><strong>P5.</strong> Simple estimate =3,600×(.22/365)×28≈$60.76, assuming constant balance and the stated day-count rule. Actual card may use average daily balance, compounding, separate APRs, grace periods and payments. Transfer balance =3,600×1.04=$3,744. Equal payment over 12 months = $312.00. Paying $250×12=$3,000 leaves $744 before subsequent interest and additional activity.</p>\n<p><strong>P6.</strong> Gross-income DTI=1,400/4,100≈34.15%. Payment/net-pay=1,400/3,250≈43.08%. Gross and net are different denominators; lenders define qualifying obligations, income, terms and applicable program requirements rather than simply using household net wages. Calculation alone cannot establish approval.</p>\n<p><strong>P7.</strong> Grading requires five grounded issues such as loan interest and compounding, actual contract guarantees/non-guarantees, risk of investment loss, lapse and possible tax consequences, unpaid loan reducing death benefit, liquidity mismatch, premiums/fees, policy and collateral terms, tax and ownership rules, regulatory suitability or promotional conflicts. Credit nuanced risk and data requests, not a blanket statement that every possible loan is illegal or automatically harmful.</p>\n</details><p><strong>Mastery gate:</strong> ≥80/100 overall; correct derivation and numerical model for P1/P2; ≥70% quantitative total; acceptable risk disclosures on P7. The entire four-lesson Module 1 concludes with a separately assessed integrated household model, not an answer-click count.</p>\n<p><strong>Sources retrieved 2026-11-01:</strong> CFPB guidance on mortgage total payments, daily-periodic credit card interest and credit agreements; SEC Investor.gov financial-planning guidance. All quoted rates, property values and loan terms in the homework are invented for training; no product approval, future return or rate offer is implied.</p>\n</section></div><h3>Primary sources</h3><ul class=\"source-list\"><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/on-a-mortgage-whats-the-difference-between-my-principal-and-interest-payment-and-my-total-monthly-payment-en-1941/\">CFPB · Monthly mortgage costs</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/how-does-my-credit-card-company-calculate-the-amount-of-interest-i-owe-en-51/\">CFPB · Card interest method</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-a-daily-periodic-rate-on-a-credit-card-en-46/\">CFPB · Daily periodic rate</a></li></ul>","capstone":"<div class=\"eyebrow\">Academic mastery</div><h1>Module 1 — Integrated Household Financial Analysis Capstone</h1>\n<p><strong>Status:</strong> Newly developed, independently checked teaching case; completion must be assessed by worked submission, not checkbox counts.<br />\n<strong>Stable lesson references:</strong> L1.S1 → financial position, L1.S2 → dated cash flow, L1.S3 → savings-growth math, L1.S4 → loans and leverage.<br />\n<strong>State context:</strong> Connecticut; fictional family, fictitious rates, income and property prices. Not the learner's personal account information.<br />\n<strong>Files:</strong> <code>data/household-base-ledger.csv</code>, <code>data/household-stress-ledger.csv</code>, <code>data/loan-amortization-36-months.csv</code>, <code>data/verified-model-answers.json</code>. The CSV ledgers are small, documented datasets intended for spreadsheet or code analysis; no bank connection or personal data is needed.</p>\n<h2>Case materials: the Vale household</h2>\n<p>It is November 1, 2026. The Vale household rents in Connecticut and wants to buy its first home. Assume all asset values and debt balances below are current as of November 1 and held within the defined household boundary. The household has one fictional recurring net paycheck of <strong>$1,450 every 14 days</strong>, beginning Friday, November 6, 2026. The next six paydays after that are Nov 20, Dec 4, Dec 18, Jan 1, Jan 15 and Jan 29. All wages post on their scheduled dates in the baseline; the stress case delays the Dec 4 deposit to Dec 11.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"text-align:left\">Opening balance sheet</th>\n<th style=\"text-align:right\">Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Checking</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$1,800</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Savings</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$4,800</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Taxable brokerage investments</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$1,500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Retirement investments</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$25,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Vehicle estimated market value</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">$14,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Auto loan outstanding</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">−$8,500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align:left\">Credit-card balance</td>\n<td style=\"text-align:right\">−$1,700</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The household makes the following fictional monthly cash payments on the same calendar day each month: rent <strong>$1,250 on the 10th</strong>; two groceries purchases <strong>$225 on the 12th and 26th</strong>; fuel <strong>$125 on the 13th and 27th</strong>; auto loan <strong>$310 on the 16th</strong>; internet <strong>$75 on the 18th</strong>; phone <strong>$60 on the 20th</strong>; card payment <strong>$50 on the 23rd</strong>; utilities <strong>$170 on the 28th</strong>; and a <strong>$100 transfer from checking to savings on the 30th</strong>. There are no new card charges, brokerage changes, investment returns, vehicle depreciation, or tax adjustments in the simplified three-month scenario. All listed dates are simulated, not payment recommendations.</p>\n<p>For a simple debt evolution model, the auto loan has 8% nominal annual interest divided by twelve and the credit card is approximated with 22% nominal annual divided by twelve. These are <strong>pedagogical simplifications</strong>: actual credit cards may accrue daily, use grace periods and multiple APRs, and lender-required amortization can differ. For each modeled month, interest is computed on the previous ending debt balance, then the scheduled payment reduces interest and principal; round interest to cents. The model assumes payments are sufficient to cover interest.</p>\n<p>A home-buying discussion introduces a hypothetical <strong>$260,000</strong> property, <strong>5% down</strong>, <strong>3% nonrecoverable closing costs</strong> and a mortgage covering the remainder of the price. No lender rate, taxes, mortgage insurance, affordability approval or assistance benefit is presumed. The potential home is only a decision alternative to evaluate, not an offer.</p>\n<h2>Required graded submission</h2>\n<p><strong>Deliverable A — Dated opening balance sheet (15 points).</strong> Identify total financial/nonfinancial assets, liabilities and net worth. Distinguish cash available today from total wealth. Identify at least two valuation uncertainties and two noncash constraints. Show the accounting equation with totals.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverable B — 3-month bank cash forecast (20 points).</strong> Use the provided base ledger to reproduce month-end combined bank cash and the minimum checking balance each month. Show why the $100 monthly savings transfer is neither new earnings nor household consumption. Reconcile the November movement in bank cash against the event ledger.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverable C — Debt roll-forward and net-worth bridge (20 points).</strong> Compute interest and principal on the vehicle and credit card for November, December and January, then reconcile February 1 net worth after three months using the simplified fixed asset valuations. Explain why a $310 auto payment cannot automatically be treated as $310 of economic expense.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverable D — Stress and liquidity defense (15 points).</strong> The Dec 4 paycheck is delayed to Dec 11 and a $1,000 car repair is paid Dec 8. Determine the maximum cash gap, identify the first time any projected checking cash becomes negative and propose two feasible responses <strong>before</strong> a bill fails. Name risks such as transfer delays or depending on additional borrowing.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverable E — Home purchase screen (15 points).</strong> Calculate down payment, mortgage starting principal, closing costs and total starting cash outlay. Compare with January 31 combined bank cash in the base case, then quantify the cash gap. Explain why the comparison is not underwriting and why taxes, insurance, moving expense, repairs and reserves matter. Would a policy loan or credit-card line create net worth if used to fund the gap? Explain.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverable F — Decision memo and evidence audit (15 points).</strong> Write a 500–800 word memo recommending one of: buy now, delay purchase, change purchase budget, or investigate verified public assistance. Include at least three quantitative figures, one downside case, one alternative, a liquidity caution and two primary sources. Use CFPB original mortgage disclosure guidance and an Investor.gov or CFPB household-finance source. Assumptions and uncertainty must be separately labeled. Any reference to a real state program must have a date-checked source rather than an invented eligibility claim.</p>\n<h2>Instructor model: independently computed results</h2>\n<p><strong>A. Opening wealth.</strong> Assets = checking 1,800 + savings 4,800 + brokerage 1,500 + retirement 25,000 + car 14,000 = <strong>$47,100</strong>. Liabilities = 8,500 + 1,700 = <strong>$10,200</strong>. Net worth = <strong>$36,900</strong>. Combined bank cash is $6,600, but not all of it is necessarily uncommitted. The car is estimated, retirement money has legal/tax access considerations and securities can fluctuate.</p>\n<p><strong>B. Cash model.</strong> Monthly external payments are $2,615: rent 1,250 + groceries 450 + gas 250 + auto payment 310 + internet 75 + phone 60 + card payment 50 + utilities 170. The $100 checking-to-savings move is internal. November receives two $1,450 wages ($2,900). Combined bank cash starts $6,600, so after November it is 6,600 +2,900−2,615 = <strong>$6,885</strong>, allocated to $1,985 checking and $4,900 savings. December: combined $7,170, checking $2,170 and savings $5,000. January has <em>three</em> paychecks totaling $4,350; combined cash grows to <strong>$8,905</strong>, checking $3,805 and savings $5,100. The base model's lowest checking balances by month are <strong>$1,205 (November), $1,450 (December) and $2,020 (January)</strong>. The student must show the dated ledger, not only totals.</p>\n<p><strong>C. Debt and net worth.</strong> November auto interest = round(8,500×.08/12,2)=$56.67; $310 payment reduces principal $253.33; auto closes at $8,246.67. Card interest approximation = round(1,700×.22/12,2)=$31.17; a $50 payment reduces card principal $18.83; card closes at $1,681.17. Total debt closes at $9,927.84. December auto/card balances close at $7,991.65 and $1,661.99. January closes at <strong>$7,734.93 auto</strong> and <strong>$1,642.46 card</strong>, total <strong>$9,377.39</strong>. Assuming unchanged brokerage/retirement/car values, January 31 total assets = $8,905 bank + $1,500 brokerage + $25,000 retirement + $14,000 car = <strong>$49,405</strong>; resulting net worth is <strong>$40,027.61</strong>. The net-worth gain of $3,127.61 corresponds to three months' earnings less consumption and modeled interest, not bank deposits alone.</p>\n<p><strong>D. Stress scenario.</strong> November remains unchanged. December still receives both checks by month-end, but one is seven days late and the surprise repair increases outside spending by $1,000. December closes at <strong>$1,170 checking</strong> and $5,000 savings, versus the base $2,170 checking. On <strong>December 10</strong>, after scheduled rent while awaiting delayed payroll, the simulated checking cash reaches <strong>−$265</strong>, the overall minimum. The bank may reject payment rather than permit negative checking. The modeled shortfall can be prevented with at least $265 <strong>cleared and accessible in checking before that debit</strong>, provided availability and transfer timing are verified, or with a lawful, documented expense/date adjustment. Because the household has savings, a prearranged transfer can be a defensible first option; relying on an unverified loan or market return is not necessary. Stress January closes at $2,805 checking and $5,100 savings. Month-end surplus alone concealed a specific missed-payment risk.</p>\n<p><strong>E. Home screen.</strong> Down payment = 260,000×.05 = <strong>$13,000</strong>; hypothetical mortgage = <strong>$247,000</strong>; nonrecoverable closing costs = 260,000×.03 = <strong>$7,800</strong>; modeled immediate cash required = <strong>$20,800</strong>. January 31 base combined cash is $8,905. Even if every dollar of checking/savings were uncommitted, <strong>cash gap = $11,895</strong>, before moving costs and reserves. The $1,500 brokerage value is market-dependent and may have tax consequences; using it does not erase the basic funding problem. A new $11,895 loan could fill an account mechanically, but would create a matching liability, additional payment obligations and no instant net worth. A 5% down payment does not establish financing approval or complete housing affordability.</p>\n<p><strong>F. Memo expectation.</strong> Given only the stated facts, 'buy now under the assumed terms' is not supported by available cash. A high-quality answer recommends delaying or changing the transaction until closing cash, post-closing reserves and total monthly cost are funded, or investigating an actual verified program with carefully stated eligibility. It quantifies the tradeoff of delaying and avoids promising appreciation, a loan approval or guaranteed compounding. An alternative recommendation can earn credit if independently shown feasible with evidence not included in this case.</p>\n<h2>Grading, source literacy and academic integrity</h2>\n<p>Total = 100 points. A passing independent submission requires <strong>at least 80 points</strong>, with <strong>at least 70% across A–E quantitative components</strong>, complete balance-sheet and cash reconciliations, and a defensible F memo. A projection without explicit dates fails the cash-timing component even if its totals are correct. A mere multiple-choice score does not satisfy this standard. An evaluator should inspect calculations and prose, allow corrections and require a short reflection describing changed reasoning.</p>\n<p>Students must not copy another household's financial information into a submission. The case data are fully simulated. Sources: https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/educator-tool/your-money-your-goals/toolkit/ ; https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/loan-estimate/ ; https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/save-and-invest/figure-out-your-finances (retrieved 2026-11-01). The case does not offer legal, tax, underwriting or financial advice.</p>\n","roadmap":"<h1>Wealth Lab — Academic Reconstruction Architecture and Prerequisite Map</h1>\n<p><strong>Working date:</strong> 2026-11-01. <strong>Status:</strong> Module 1 academic candidate pack, NOT a verified production deployment.<br />\n<strong>Source of audit findings:</strong> user's <code>wealth-lab-curriculum-depth-audit.md</code> dated 2026-11-01.<br />\n<strong>Current-source verification:</strong> GitHub repository <code>mvolkert84/wealth-lab</code>, <code>main</code> branch inspected 2026-11-01. M1 has four <code>academy-chapters/L1.S*-edition2.js</code> packages and compatible Foundation calculator code. The review found substantive Edition 2 work, so the audit's older preview cannot be taken as the current live version. The repository's registered lesson engine expects legacy question positions to remain fixed.</p>\n<h2>Why this reconstruction is necessary</h2>\n<p>The audit found 59 original lesson IDs across 16 modules, and observed a standard 12-part shape in its available advanced lesson pack. It warns that structural conformity, many short MCQs and attractive calculators can conceal missing economic mechanisms, formal derivations, evidence evaluation and independent judgment. The current M1 repository adds about 4,000 words per edition-2 chapter but still uses the fixed 12-part convention. A strong replacement standard must avoid a new fixed number of sections; it must test demonstrated outcomes, not page count.</p>\n<p>This package is <strong>additive content</strong>, not a replacement of the current Edition 2 documents. The current material supplies many plain-language explanations and introductory checks. The new M1 documents supply formal models, derivations, independent written homework and a reproducible multi-period capstone. Neither academic completeness nor live-app integration is claimed solely on the existence of these files.</p>\n<h2>Four-lesson foundation: outcome and gap map</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>ID</th>\n<th>Existing topic and learning base</th>\n<th>Gap addressed by new academic layer</th>\n<th>Newly built evidence</th>\n<th>Later connections</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>L1.S1</td>\n<td>Wealth, assets, liabilities, home equity and liquidity; original Edition 2 numerical workbook</td>\n<td>Formal personal statement, asset valuation method, stocks/flows distinction, detailed transaction ledger, ratios, defendable net-worth bridge</td>\n<td>Statement with evidence standards; full transaction bridge; seven open-response graded problems</td>\n<td>Accounting (M8), mortgage LTV (M2), estate balance sheets (M3/M13)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L1.S2</td>\n<td>Payday sequencing, bill timing, expenses and reserve logic</td>\n<td>Classified cash-flow statement and reconciliation, 26/12 paycheck mismatch, cash-flow floor, multi-month stress</td>\n<td>Three-way bridge between cash/debt/equity; dated ledger; source-supported timing case; six graded problems</td>\n<td>Financial modeling, underwriting M2, corporate cash planning M15</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L1.S3</td>\n<td>Growth, investment horizons, fee/return comparisons</td>\n<td>Proof of PV/FV/annuity formulas, APY conversion, inflation Fisher relation, NPV with uncertain payoffs</td>\n<td>Formula derivations, numeric sensitivities, seven graded problems, 0%-rate checks</td>\n<td>M7 TVM, bond pricing M9, equity valuation M8, retirement M12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>L1.S4</td>\n<td>Consumer debt, leverage and installment payments</td>\n<td>Amortization derivation, day-count caveats, debt service, collateral leverage, stress survival</td>\n<td>Payment schedule, 0%-rate boundary, comparative rates, seven graded problems</td>\n<td>Mortgage financing M2, business leverage M15, insurance policy loans M13</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3>Formal foundation prerequisites that may need remedial lessons</h3>\n<p><strong>P0-A — Practical algebra:</strong> fractions, signed cash flows, percentages, powers, parentheses, and solving one-variable equations. Provide optional short remediation, then assess on worked problems. This can be embedded before L1.S3 without changing L1.S3 ID.</p>\n<p><strong>P0-B — Spreadsheet literacy:</strong> columns, dates, absolute/relative cell references, SUM, validation, rounding, scenario labels and reconciliations. Teach within M1 capstone, then require a live model in M7/M8.</p>\n<p><strong>P0-C — Financial source literacy:</strong> account statement versus bank balance, primary regulatory source versus sponsored advertisement, retrieval date, annual and monthly conventions. Begin in L1.S1, continue in every module.</p>\n<p><strong>P0-D — Risk language:</strong> distinguish a deterministic worked example from a stochastic forecast, a contractual payment from a return expectation, and a risk parameter from an empirical probability. Introduce in L1.S3 and L1.S4; formal statistics comes later.</p>\n<h2>Proposed 16-module academic prerequisite sequence</h2>\n<p>This is a <strong>proposed outcome map</strong>, not a claim that current lessons teach all these competencies. Keep IDs L1.S1–L16.S4 in the 59-lesson map wherever possible. Insert bridge units as <em>subsections or ungraded foundation activities first</em> to avoid overwriting existing progress; consider stable new lesson IDs only after a full content-gap review and a migration plan.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Module</th>\n<th>Prerequisite knowledge</th>\n<th>Competency that must be demonstrated</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>M1 Foundations</td>\n<td>Practical arithmetic</td>\n<td>Reconciled household assets/debts/flows, future-value calculation, loan model, defendable cash forecast</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M2 Real Estate &amp; Mortgages</td>\n<td>M1, basic algebra</td>\n<td>Price/loan/cash-to-close statements, amortization, underwriting measures, refinance and buy/rent models</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M3 Trusts &amp; Inheritance</td>\n<td>M1, M2 valuation foundations</td>\n<td>Separate legal title, beneficiary claims, debt, fiduciary duty and probate from financial intuition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M4 Investing &amp; Growth</td>\n<td>M1, basic probability concepts</td>\n<td>Explain ownership, security costs, returns and downside with sourced calculations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M5 Insurance &amp; Protection</td>\n<td>M1; basic contract literacy</td>\n<td>Calculate coverage gaps and interpret exclusions, guarantees, reserves and contingent risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M6 Taxes &amp; Advanced Strategies</td>\n<td>M1, M3–M5 case foundations</td>\n<td>Assemble simple tax mechanics with year-specific source hierarchy and legal limitations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M7 Economics &amp; Financial Math</td>\n<td>M1 S3/S4, algebra, spreadsheet literacy</td>\n<td>Time-value derivations; micro/macro mechanisms; data and rates analysis; discounting and real returns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M8 Stocks &amp; Company Analysis</td>\n<td>M7, <strong>bridge: financial accounting</strong></td>\n<td>Reconcile three formal statements, calculate ratios, forecast free cash flow, value enterprise/equity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M9 Bonds, Funds &amp; Alternatives</td>\n<td>M7 and credit-risk foundation</td>\n<td>Discount bonds, measure yields and duration; compare security mechanics, cost and counterparties</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M10 Portfolio Design &amp; Risk</td>\n<td>M4, M7, <strong>bridge: statistics</strong></td>\n<td>Probability distributions, covariance, portfolio variance, efficient allocation, drawdowns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M11 Income &amp; Investment Taxes</td>\n<td>M6, M8, M9</td>\n<td>Reproducible tax-return case with lots, holding periods, state/federal differences and compliance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M12 Retirement &amp; Tax-Advantaged Accounts</td>\n<td>M7, M10, M11</td>\n<td>Retirement cash-flow forecast, account selection, inflation/RMD considerations and sequence stress</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M13 Insurance &amp; Estate Design</td>\n<td>M3, M5, M6, M11, M12</td>\n<td>Coverage/estate integration, trust funding and legal ownership analysis; no 'free-loan' mythology</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M14 Rental Property &amp; Real Estate Investing</td>\n<td>M2, M7, M8, M11</td>\n<td>Cash-on-cash, NOI, cap rates, DSCR, depreciation and sale economics in integrated model</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M15 Business Ownership &amp; Complex Strategies</td>\n<td>M7, M8, M10, M11, <strong>bridge: managerial accounting</strong></td>\n<td>Working-capital models, corporate finance, capital budgeting, entity economics and ethics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M16 Adviser Practice &amp; Capstone</td>\n<td>Mastery of M1–M15</td>\n<td>Full sourced financial plan; alternatives, quantitative stress, ethics, limitations, professional memo</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>Missing disciplines — detailed placement proposal</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Financial accounting:</strong> a discrete assessed multi-part bridge <em>before M8.S2</em>, with ledger, accruals, income statement, balance sheet, cash flow, depreciation and working-capital reconciliations. Some early personal concepts are analogies, not substitutes for GAAP accounting.</li>\n<li><strong>Managerial accounting:</strong> add cost behavior, contribution margin, break-even, cost allocation and budget variance between M8 and M15; grade a spreadsheet model.</li>\n<li><strong>Statistics and regression:</strong> add probability, expected return, sample variance, normal and fat-tail behavior, covariance, correlation, regression assumptions and estimation error as M7→M10 bridge.</li>\n<li><strong>Financial modeling:</strong> teach formal model architecture, assumptions, checks, scenarios, reproducibility, sensitivity tables and error auditing in M1, deepen M7, require M8 and M16 independently graded files.</li>\n<li><strong>Micro- and macroeconomics:</strong> extend M7 beyond inflation and central bank summaries to supply/demand, incentives, opportunity cost, GDP identity, cycles, transmission, labor markets and open-economy effects.</li>\n<li><strong>Corporate finance:</strong> teach capital budgeting, WACC derivations, capital structure, working capital and capital allocation explicitly in M8 and M15, without treating household loans as a sufficient basis.</li>\n<li><strong>Derivatives and hedging:</strong> add assessed futures, forwards, options, payoffs, margin, convexity, hedging effectiveness and counterparty risk to M9/M10; introduce no trading recommendation as inherently safe.</li>\n<li><strong>Banking and market structure:</strong> add deposit creation, payment systems, custody, broker-dealer structure, settlement, regulation and crises across M7–M9.</li>\n<li><strong>Business law and ethics:</strong> cover contracts, fiduciary conflicts, consumer protection, compliance and recordkeeping within M6, M13, M15 and M16, with primary legal and professional standards.</li>\n<li><strong>Behavioral finance:</strong> assess sunk-cost fallacy, loss aversion, anchoring, overconfidence, financial stress and decision-process controls in M4/M10/M16.</li>\n<li><strong>International finance:</strong> add currency quotation, parity and exchange-rate exposure, international diversification and macro feedback in M7/M9/M10.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Avoid creating a patchwork of one-paragraph 'mentions'. Each bridge needs prerequisites, primary sources, derivations where appropriate, independently graded quantitative/written submissions, worked solutions and a placement decision. The final placement depends on reviewing the latest verified source before modifying any module.</p>\n<h2>Backward-compatible integration contract</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Preserve all stable <code>L*.S*</code> lesson IDs, original Edition 2 part sequence, original part-question order, chapter-question order, existing local storage key <code>wealthlab-state-v1</code>, navigation, typography and colors.</li>\n<li>Do not replace or reindex <code>TEXTBOOK[id].pages</code> or <code>PART_CHECKS[id]</code> for prior questions. New academic blocks can be <strong>separate keyed additive data</strong> under <code>window.WealthLabAcademicModules[id]</code> until the live reader explicitly supports new section IDs and graded written work.</li>\n<li>Version the educational content independently from app shell deployment. Use fully namespaced, durable academic section IDs such as <code>L1.S1.A1</code> for a new section; never silently reuse an old legacy question ID for a different answer.</li>\n<li>Introduce rubric and instructor-key metadata without automatically writing grades into legacy progress records. An older recorded completion remains historically visible; new academic mastery is a separate, non-destructive record pending learner submission and review.</li>\n<li>The preview's numerical labs are isolated, <strong>local-only, ephemeral</strong> and cannot access bank accounts, send numbers over the internet or alter stored Wealth Lab data. A production implementation requires privacy, accessibility and offline regression tests.</li>\n<li>Before a production merge, fetch latest commit/tree and compare with all four current M1 edition files; run <code>scripts/check-gold-foundations.mjs</code>, relevant reader checks, offline service-worker checks and manual mobile visual review. Restore from baseline if any question positions or progress keys shift.</li>\n<li>Current pack is prepared for merge, <strong>not merged or published to the live app</strong>. The user explicitly prohibited overwriting the live app with older preview files. This requirement is satisfied by separate local deliverables.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Editorial exit gate</h2>\n<p>Before calling Module 1 academic-complete: review accuracy and source freshness; obtain independent written attempts for each lesson; verify model cells and numeric answers; inspect minimum dated bank balances; grade the capstone with rubric; test accessibility/reader UX in the current app; and demonstrate that old progress, notes, bookmarks, quiz responses and routes survive the change. A 12-part count or word threshold is not an exit criterion.</p>\n<p><strong>Academic limitations:</strong> Wealth Lab is independent self-study, not accredited higher education, degree credit, CFP Board registration or CFA Institute materials. Professional licensing, tax advice and legal advice require distinct qualifications and context.</p>\n"};
(function bootstrap(){
 "use strict";
 const docs=window.WLAcademicDocuments;
 const idPattern=/^L1\.S[1-4]$/;
 const fmt=(n)=>Number.isFinite(n)?new Intl.NumberFormat("en-US",{style:"currency",currency:"USD"}).format(n):"Check inputs";
 function evaluate(lab){
  const v={};for(const i of lab.querySelectorAll("[data-v]"))v[i.dataset.v]=Number(i.value);
  const o={},id=lab.dataset.lab;
  if(id==="L1.S1"){o.assets=v.cash+v.home+v.other;o.liabilities=v.mort+v.debt;o.networth=o.assets-o.liabilities;o.stress=o.networth+v.home*v.shock/100;}
  if(id==="L1.S2"){o.floor=v.opening-v.rent;o.needed=Math.max(0,-o.floor);o.end=o.floor+v.pay-v.bills;}
  if(id==="L1.S3"){const n=Math.round(v.years*12),r=v.rate/1200;if(n<=0||r<=-1||v.years>100){o.future=NaN;o.contrib=NaN;o.growth=NaN;o.real=NaN;}else{o.future=Math.abs(r)<1e-10?v.deposit*n:v.deposit*Math.expm1(n*Math.log1p(r))/r;o.contrib=v.deposit*n;o.growth=o.future-o.contrib;o.real=v.inflation<=-100?NaN:o.future/Math.pow(1+v.inflation/100,v.years);}}
  if(id==="L1.S4"){const n=Math.round(v.months),r=v.rate/1200;if(n<=0||r<=-1||v.principal<0||n>1200){o.payment=NaN;o.firstint=NaN;o.firstprinc=NaN;o.totalint=NaN;}else{o.payment=Math.abs(r)<1e-10?v.principal/n:v.principal*r/(1-Math.pow(1+r,-n));o.firstint=v.principal*r;o.firstprinc=o.payment-o.firstint;o.totalint=o.payment*n-v.principal;}}
  for(const el of lab.querySelectorAll("[data-out]"))el.textContent=fmt(o[el.dataset.out]);
 }
 window.WealthLabAcademicInit=()=>{
  for(const lab of document.querySelectorAll(".wl-academic [data-lab]"))evaluate(lab);
 };
 document.addEventListener("input",e=>{
  const lab=e.target.closest?.(".wl-academic [data-lab]");
  if(lab&&e.target.matches("[data-v]"))evaluate(lab);
 });
 window.WealthLabAcademicMarkup=(id)=>{
  if(!idPattern.test(id)||!docs[id])return '<div class="article">Academic content unavailable for this lesson.</div>';
  const notes='<p class="wl-acad-foot">Independent university-style self-study content; not accredited college credit. Examples are fictional, and calculators do not save inputs. For your previous chapter answers and personal notes, use the original Learn, Try It and Notes tabs.</p>';
  const capstone=id==="L1.S4"?'<details class="wl-acad-capstone"><summary>Module 1 · Cumulative capstone and course roadmap</summary><div>'+docs.capstone+docs.roadmap+'</div></details>':'';
  return '<div class="article wl-academic" aria-label="University-style finance textbook supplement"><p class="wl-acad-kicker">FOUNDATIONS · ACADEMIC TEXTBOOK EXPANSION</p><p class="wl-acad-explain">Read the complete lesson below, work through the calculations, and solve the open-response problems. Original chapter progress and bookmarks remain separate and unchanged.</p>'+docs[id]+capstone+notes+'</div>';
 };
})();
